NEWS for veteran Window Maker users
-----------------------------------

-- next

Hot corner action chooser
-------------------------

The Hot Corner Shortcut Preferences panel replaces the free-form command
fields with per-corner action pop-ups listing the built-in actions for
showing the desktop, showing all windows, minimizing all windows,
switching workspaces and locking the screen. A final "Custom action..."
entry accepts any key binding name or shell command, so existing
configurations keep working unchanged.

Extra mouse button bindings
---------------------------

Buttons 10 and higher can be bound to root-window mouse actions through
the new ExtraMouseButtons defaults array, for example
`("10 SelectWindows", "11 MoveToNextWorkspace")`. There is no WPrefs UI.
The key is intended for expert configuration. Buttons 1-9 keep their
existing dedicated bindings.

Auto-hide the cursor
--------------------

AutoHideCursor hides the pointer after non-modifier keyboard input or
after the pointer has been idle, and shows it again on intentional
movement, a button press, or the wheel. The defaults are
AutoHideCursor (master switch, off by default),
HideCursorAfterKeyboard (on by default), HideCursorAfterIdle (off by
default) and HideCursorIdleDelay (milliseconds, 1000..3600000,
default 5000). The feature uses XFixes plus optional XInput2 raw
events, so an idle desktop gains no timers or event selections while
it is disabled. The master switch is also available in the Expert User
Preferences panel.

Find the mouse pointer
----------------------

A new FindMouse action highlights the pointer with a ring that follows
it for a moment. Bind FindMouseKey (unbound by default) or run
`wmakermsg action FindMouse`. The ring is click-through and works
without a compositor, and it uses the focused titlebar's text and
background colours. FindMouseDuration sets the lifetime in milliseconds
(default 1000). FindMouseRadius sets the ring radius in pixels. The
default 0 sizes it from the monitor under the pointer (one twelfth of
its smaller side, clamped to 32..1024). A positive value overrides that
with an explicit pixel radius.

Accent Picker
-------------------

Builds configured with `--enable-xkb-multilingual` gain an opt-in
Accent Picker for accented letters, currency signs, and other special
characters. Both triggers ship disabled so an upgrade never takes a key
away from applications. The recommended first setting is
`AccentPickerKey = Menu`: hold Menu, press a base key, tap the same key
again to cycle the variants, and release Menu to insert the selection.
Escape cancels. A dedicated modifier can be used instead with
`AccentPickerModifier`, for example CapsLock mapped to Hyper with
`setxkbmap -option caps:hyper`. `AccentPickerSets` chooses the character
sets, defaulting to `("auto", "Currency")`, and `auto` derives language
sets from all configured XKB layouts. IPA is never selected by `auto`.
Per-base overrides can be added with the `AccentPickerVariants`
dictionary. The candidate popup lays variants out horizontally, clicking
a variant inserts it immediately, and clicking outside cancels.
`--enable-modelock` remains accepted as a deprecated alias for the
canonical configure option.

Workspace layout menu
---------------------

The Workspaces root menu gains a Layout submenu listing Float, Tile,
Tile wide, Grid and Monocle. Picking an entry sets the tiling layout of
the current workspace, exactly like the LayoutCycle, LayoutAxis and
LayoutFloat key bindings or the `wmakermsg set-layout` command, and a
diamond marks the layout the workspace currently uses. The marker
follows workspace switches, so the same submenu always reflects the
workspace you are on. The current-workspace marker in the Workspaces
list itself, lost in an indicator-drawing rework, is drawn again.

Load Default in the menu editor
-------------------------------

The Applications Menu Definition panel gains a "Load Default" link in
its instructions block. Clicking it, after confirmation, rebuilds the
menu being edited from the shipped default menu file and the next
Save writes the restored default to the menu file currently in use.

Reset Defaults in the main window
---------------------------------

A Reset Defaults command button backs up the WindowMaker, WMSound,
WMGLOBAL and WMRootMenu domain files as .wprefs-backup siblings,
replaces them with the shipped defaults, refreshes every initialized
preferences panel through its undo callback and restarts the window
manager over IPC.

HEIF image support
------------------

WRaster can now optionally load HEIF images when the libheif library
(1.15 or newer) is available, detected automatically at configure time
like the other format libraries and disableable with
`configure --disable-heif`. One loader covers the whole ISO-BMFF image
family: `.heic` files (HEVC coded), `.avif` files (AV1 coded) and the
generic multi-image brands, so high-resolution root backgrounds and
icon assets in modern formats work everywhere WRaster is used
(`wmsetbg`, icons, themes, `wmiv`). Only the HEIF image file-type
brands are recognised, so MP4 and other ISO-BMFF media files keep
falling through to the unknown-format path. Multi-image files (as
produced by iPhone bursts) load per frame like GIF and TIFF, and
decoding honors the container's pixel aspect and rotation metadata.

Dock orientation
----------------

The dock can be pinned to any of the four screen edges with the new
`DockEdge` domain key (`Left`, `Right`, `Top` or `Bottom`, default
`Right`) and the Dock position radios in WPrefs. A top or bottom dock is
a NeXTSTEP-style shelf: a wide monitor no longer gives up a column of
its width, and the icon yard rides one row above a bottom dock instead
of losing a column. Dragging the dock to another edge snaps it there
with hysteresis, `WMState` remembers the edge across restarts, drawers
open perpendicular to the dock on every edge, and `PlaceIcon`,
`wArrangeIcons` and the usable-area computation follow the dock's edge.

With `NoWindowOverDock` set, the reservation is now also published to
other clients as `_NET_WM_STRUT` and `_NET_WM_STRUT_PARTIAL` on the
info window, covering only the head the dock touches. Wmaker's own
placement keeps using its internal inset, and `_NET_WORKAREA` still does
not include the dock. A dock on an inner monitor edge has no external
strut, since EWMH struts must extend from a root-screen edge.

The `dock-launch SLOT` slot and the `child_exit` event slot are now the
dock's along-axis index rather than always the Y cell: they equal the
old value while the dock is on a left or right edge, and count along X
on a top or bottom dock. The new `edge` field in the `appicon` dict of
`get-window-tree` tells clients which axis the slot counts on.

Per-connection `WDisplay` context
---------------------------------

The window manager's connection-scoped runtime state now lives in a single
`WDisplay` object (`src/wdisplay.c`) instead of the `w_global` struct and the
`wPreferences` runtime fields: interned atoms, the window-to-object hash
tables, extension bases, event timestamps, the crossing-suppression ring, the
screen array, and the runtime flags, cursors and modifier state. `WScreen`
gains a back-pointer to the context. This is an internal refactor with no
user-visible change, except that the switch-panel/workspace-map textures and
the clamped window-placement origin are now realised per screen, fixing a
latent multi-screen bug where they were built from whichever screen applied
the configuration last. The empty `wPreferences` shell is retired and the
monitor's fallback window-manager list moved into `monitor.c`, its only
consumer.

Generic action IPC verb
-----------------------

`wmakermsg action NAME` runs any key-binding action by name, so scripts
and daemons reach every action a key binding can trigger without a
dedicated verb per action. The name is the short action form
(NextWorkspace) or the config key (NextWorkspaceKey), optionally followed
by a window id, and `wmakermsg info` now lists every accepted name. New
window-manager actions go through `action` instead of new verbs.
Hot-corner entries whose value matches an action name now run that action
directly instead of shelling out, while unknown strings still run as
shell commands.

The `show-desktop`, `grow-to-edge`, `shrink-to-edge`, `show-all`,
`arrange-icons`, `move-to-edge`, `hide-application`, `hide-others` and
`window-close` verbs are removed in favour of the `ShowDesktop`,
`GrowToEdge{Left,Right,Up,Down}`, `ShrinkToEdge{Left,Right,Up,Down}`,
`ShowAll`, `ArrangeIcons`, `MoveToEdge{Left,Right,Up,Down}`, `Hide`,
`HideOthers` and `Close` actions, reached through `action`. The new
`MoveToEdge*NoSnap` actions cover the former `nosnap` form, and the
`Hide`/`HideOthers`/`Close` actions now accept a window id to target a
background window, matching the verbs they replace.

Key bindings over IPC and a shortcuts panel
-------------------------------------------

`wmakermsg get-keybinds` lists every active key binding as a `keybinds`
array. Each row gives the short action name that `action` accepts, the
configuration key, the raw configuration spec, and whether the binding is
active, plus the rendered display label for bound rows. Unbound rows are
omitted by default and included with `get-keybinds all`. The same data
drives a new non-modal `Keyboard Shortcuts` panel, opened from a root-menu
`KEYBIND_PANEL` entry or the bindable `ShowKeybinds` action, so the panel
appears in its own list. The panel is read-only; WPrefs remains the
editor.

Touchpad gesture daemon
-----------------------

The new optional `wmtouchd` daemon (`configure --with-libinput`) reads
three- to five-finger touchpad swipes and two- to five-finger pinches
through libinput and delivers them through the `action` verb. The gesture-to-action mapping
lives in the `WMTouch` defaults domain and is edited with `wdwrite`, for
example:

  {
      SwipeThreshold      = 60;
      PinchThreshold      = 1.5;
      ThreeFingerLeft     = "PrevWorkspace";
      ThreeFingerRight    = "NextWorkspace";
      ThreeFingerUp       = "ShowDesktop";
      ThreeFingerDown     = "None";
      ThreeFingerPinchOut = "Maximize";
      ThreeFingerPinchIn  = "Miniaturize";
  }

A gesture bound to `None`, empty, or an absent key does nothing, so the
domain opts in per gesture; `wmtouchd --check` validates the names against
the window manager without executing anything. It links libinput, libudev
and libWUtil, never X11.
Opening the touchpad needs device access for the user running the daemon:
a logind uaccess ACL, the `input` group, or a udev rule on the device, as
described in wmtouchd(1).

Window tree introspection over IPC
----------------------------------

`wmakermsg get_window_tree` dumps the full window-manager state as a
JSON tree: applications with their windows, tab-group membership and
the active member, stacking level and index, dock/clip attachment and
slot, frame geometry, client PID, and the complete state flags
(focus, shade, hide, maximize axes, fullscreen, urgency, modality).
The reply needs no X round-trips, so bars, switchers, session tools
and test harnesses can read it with `wmakermsg get_window_tree | jq .`
instead of scraping EWMH root properties. The new `--json` option
converts any other reply (`get_windows`, `get_workspaces`, a command
result) from property-list text to JSON as well, and is implied by
`get_window_tree`.

Comments in defaults files
--------------------------

The defaults file reader now skips C-style comments, so both block
comments (/* ... */) and line comments (// to end of line) may be used
to document keys in the WMGLOBAL template and in user domain files.
Comments are not recognized inside quoted strings, and an unterminated
block comment still rejects the file.

Configurable WINGs system colors
--------------------------------

The WMGLOBAL domain gains eight color keys that override the hard-coded
WINGs system colors, so the chrome of every WINGs-based application
(WPrefs, the workspace menu, the switch panel, the info panel) can be
themed from one file without touching any code. Edit the domain file
(~/GNUstep/Defaults/WMGLOBAL, or ~/.config/WindowMaker/WMGLOBAL) and
add the keys with X11 color names or #rrggbb values:

  DefaultBackColor - the default widget surface (the gray chrome) and
    most relief lines derived from it
  DarkGrayColor   - disabled text and the sunken relief lines
  TabColor        - the tab view's tab surface
  TabLightColor   - the tab view's light edge
  SelectionColor  - the table view's selected row
  GridColor       - the table view's grid lines
  TextFieldBackColor - the text input field surface
  SelectedBackColor - the background of selected states: the selected
    list row, the popup menu highlight and the pressed or lit button

For example:

  DefaultBackColor = "#303030";
  DarkGrayColor = "#1f1f1f";

Values fall back to the built-in grays when unset or unparseable.
Window Maker watches the domain and re-applies changed colors to its
WINGs windows live, in place, without restarting. Standalone WINGs
applications pick the keys up at their next start. The shipped
WMGLOBAL template documents the keys as commented-out examples with
their fallbacks.

Per-window window-menu Options toggles
--------------------------------------

The window menu's Options submenu gains four new entries. "Skip window
list" and "Skip switch panel" hide the window they are toggled on from
the window list and from the switch panel (Alt-Tab), and "Lock
position" and "Lock size" pin it against moving and resizing. A locked
dimension also ignores the window's own ConfigureRequest for that axis,
so a locked terminal that changes font no longer resizes itself.

The skip attributes could previously only be set from the window
Inspector, where "Save" writes them under the window specification
(instance, class or all windows), so hiding a single window of an
application while it runs was impossible. The lock flags could only be
set by clients. The menu toggles are per-window: they affect the window
they are invoked on and never touch other windows of the same
application. Like "Keep on top" and "Keep at bottom" the skip toggles
are live only. The locks survive a warm restart (`wmakermsg restart` or
`--replace`) through the per-window state property. None of the four is
written to the session file, which restores entries by instance, class
and command and could hand the value to a sibling the user never chose.
The Inspector keeps working as before and remains the way to make a
setting permanent for an application.

Xlib is gone, everything runs on xcb
-------------------------------------

Window Maker, libWINGs, WPrefs and the utility programs no longer
link libX11: property and hint requests, event delivery, grabs,
colours, cursors, selections and cut buffers all go through libxcb,
and the keyboard is driven entirely by libxkbcommon. Text fields
talk to fcitx5 or ibus servers through xcb-imdkit when available,
with an xkbcommon compose-table fallback. Property reads at startup
are batched into a single server round-trip, so taking over a busy
desktop is noticeably faster, especially over a remote display.
Nothing changes for users: the same themes, defaults, menus and key
bindings keep working, and the only X11 headers left in the tree are
the ones used by the optional XSMP session-manager module.

Xinerama multi-head support is unaffected by the removal of Xlib's
libXinerama: head geometry comes from RandR 1.5 GetMonitors on every
modern server, and servers that expose Xinerama without a RandR
description (nested servers, builds configured --disable-randr) fall
back to the XINERAMA extension through the xcb-xinerama module, the
generated xcb binding that libxcb ships.

XPM without libXpm
-------------------

libXpm is no longer used or required: XPM images are decoded by the
reader built into libwraster, so the library no longer pulls libXpm
(and through it libX11) into its link. The reader now accepts what
real-world theme files use: comments and blank lines anywhere in the
file, symbolic and monochrome colour entries, one to four characters
per pixel, named colours ("white", "gray50", ...) and grayscale
"%nn" values. Loading still fails cleanly on truncated rows and
unresolvable colours instead of rendering garbage. The
"--disable-xpm" configure option is gone (XPM is always built in).

Urgent titlebar blink
----------------------

A window that advertises urgency (WM_HINTS UrgencyHint or
_NET_WM_STATE_DEMANDS_ATTENTION) now blinks its titlebar between the
focused and unfocused appearance until the urgency is cleared or the
window gains focus. Before this the only urgency feedback was the
dock/appicon bounce, which users with a hidden dock never saw. The
blink toggles the frame's focused look every 500 ms (a 1 s full
cycle): the titlebar texture, bevel and text, the button cores, the
tab segments, the outer border and the inner-border strips all follow
the phase. No texture is re-rendered per tick, both states are
pre-rendered and cached, so a tick only re-points the window background
and repaints. The blink never runs on a focused, miniaturized, hidden
or GNUstep window. It is enabled by default and can be turned off with
"BlinkTitlebarWhenUrgent = NO" in ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker.

Configurable switch panel ordering
-----------------------------------

A new SwitcherOrder option chooses how the switch panel (Alt-Tab) and
the window list menu order their entries. The default "Focus" keeps
the most-recently-focused ordering. "Age" lists windows oldest-created
first, independent of the focus history, so the cycle order stays
stable while windows are opened and closed. The window list menu
applies the same ordering within each workspace block. The setting is
runtime-reloadable and has an Expert panel checkbox in WPrefs.

Shared-edge resize of tiled half/quarter windows
-------------------------------------------------

When two windows form a left/right or top/bottom tiled pair (or four
windows form a 2x2 quarter grid), resizing through the shared edge now
moves the seam instead of resizing one window: the grabbed window grows
by the drag amount while its partner shrinks by the same amount, keeping
the tiling intact. Pairing is automatic and geometry based -- any two resizable
windows that abut across a shared edge and match on the perpendicular
axis qualify, however they were created or positioned. Diagonal grabs
pair each axis independently, so dragging the
inner corner of a 2x2 grid moves both seams at once. A completed paired
resize clears the maximize state of every window it moved, so a later
titlebar drag just moves it. The feature is off by default; enable it
with "SharedEdgeResize = YES" in ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker.
Pairing tolerates hand placement: facing edges within max(WindowGap,
8) pixels and perpendicular extents within an eighth of the smaller
one pair. The seam gap is preserved during the drag.

Clipboard history expiry and persistence for wmclipd
----------------------------------------------------

The `wmclipd` clipboard daemon accepts a new `--ttl DURATION` option
(default 0, disabled) that removes history entries after a fixed age.
Durations use user-friendly units: `30s`, `10m`, `2h`, `1d`, a bare
number means seconds, combinations such as `1h30m` are accepted, the
smallest enabled duration is `5s` and the largest is `365d`. Expiry
only affects the history ring, the live CLIPBOARD payload is preserved.
Pinned entries are exempt from expiry (matching capacity eviction) and
re-copying the same content restarts that entry's timer. Expiry is
driven by the daemon's main poll loop, so entries disappear on time
even without new clipboard activity.

The history ring can also survive daemon restarts with the new
`--persist` option. On a clean exit (`SIGTERM`/`SIGINT`, as sent by the
login manager at session logout, or an X server disconnect) the ring is
written to a single binary state file, and the next start restores
content, order, pin state, capture time and content type. Selection
ownership, X timestamps and TTL deadlines stay session-local. Because
clipboard history regularly contains passwords and other secrets,
persistence is opt-in: without the flag nothing is written to disk.
The state file is created with mode 0600 in a 0700 directory under
`$XDG_STATE_HOME/WindowMaker/` (fallback
`~/.local/state/WindowMaker/`), and `--history-file PATH` chooses a
different location. Screenshot entries whose backing file is gone and
non-pinned entries already past their `--ttl` are skipped on load, and
restored entries never re-claim the live CLIPBOARD selection. In the
popup, entries captured on a previous day are labelled with the
weekday and day instead of a bare time.

Sound event support restored
-----------------------------

Window Maker can again emit sound events for window actions (shade,
unshade, maximize, unmaximize, iconify, deiconify, hide, unhide, dock,
undock, kaboom) and lifecycle events (appstart, appexit, startup,
shutdown, workspace, screenshot, lock). Events travel on the existing AF_UNIX IPC stream
as a new `sound` event type, not the spoofable X ClientMessage used by
the original 2001-era implementation. A new `DisableSound` preference
(default YES, opt-in) gates the 20 automatic emit sites. The
`play-sound` IPC verb emits events on demand and deliberately bypasses
the preference for testing and scripting. The WPrefs "Other
Configurations" panel has a "Sounds" toggle in the "Animations and
Sound" frame. A new `wmsoundd` daemon (built when PipeWire and libsndfile are found,
`--with-pipewire=auto`) subscribes to the IPC stream, reads the
`WMSound` defaults domain to map event names to sound files, and plays
them through PipeWire. libsndfile decodes WAV/AIFF/AU/FLAC/Ogg/Vorbis/
Opus/MP3, so the freedesktop sound theme files under
`/usr/share/sounds/` are usable directly. wmaker itself never links
PipeWire or libsndfile.

Skip empty workspaces when switching workspaces
-----------------------------------------------

A new "Skip empty workspaces" option (SkipEmptyWorkspaces, default off,
in the WPrefs Expert panel) makes the Next/Prev/Left/Right/Up/Down
workspace shortcuts, the Clip scroll-wheel and arrow buttons, the
root-window mouse-wheel switch actions, the workspace-map directional
keys, and the `workspace next`/`workspace prev` IPC verbs skip past
workspaces that contain no non-omnipresent windows. Explicit jumps
(Workspace1..10, LastWorkspace, Next/PrevWorkspaceLayerKey, `workspace N`,
`workspace last`, workspace-menu entries, and pager-sent
_NET_CURRENT_DESKTOP messages) and the MoveTo* actions that send a window
to another workspace are unaffected. While the option is on,
AdvanceToNewWorkspace is suppressed for the gated actions (so cycling
never lands on a freshly-created empty workspace). CycleWorkspaces still
wraps the walk within the existing set. AutoRemoveEmptyWorkspaces is
orthogonal and both options may be combined.

Show Desktop key binding (ShowDesktopKey)
------------------------------------------

A new keyboard shortcut, ShowDesktopKey (default unbound), toggles
the EWMH "showing desktop" state: it iconifies all non-skip windows
across all workspaces (setting _NET_SHOWING_DESKTOP to 1 on the root
window), and a second press restores them. An external
_NET_SHOWING_DESKTOP ClientMessage from a panel still toggles the same
state, so the two paths share one restore list. Also available as the
IPC verb `show-desktop`.

Edge-snap window actions (MoveToEdge, GrowToEdge, ShrinkToEdge)
----------------------------------------------------------------

Three new window actions that move, grow or shrink the focused
window toward a screen edge, stopping at the nearest intervening
window edge on the way. All three take a direction (left, right,
up, down) and are available as keyboard shortcuts, window menu
entries and IPC verbs (move-to-edge, grow-to-edge,
shrink-to-edge). MoveToEdge also accepts an optional snap/nosnap
argument to control whether it stops at neighbor windows. A
WindowGap setting controls the pixel margin left between the
snapped window and screen/window edges (0 = flush).

Application-group switch panel sub-panel (drill)
-------------------------------------------------

The app-group switch panel now supports a sub-panel for
drilling into a group's individual windows. While the app-group panel
(GroupNextKey, default Mod4+Tab) is open, pressing Down or grave opens a
second icon strip below the group strip showing the windows of the
highlighted application, and moves the panel focus to it. Up moves
focus back to the group strip and collapses the sub-panel. Left/Right
navigate whichever strip currently has focus: groups on the main strip,
individual windows on the sub strip. Tab/Shift+Tab always switch
groups. Home/End and letter keys act on the focused strip (letters
match window titles in the sub strip, WM_CLASS on the main strip). No
new key binding is needed -- the drill keys are handled inside the open
panel, so the top-level Mod4+grave (GroupWindowNextKey) binding is
unaffected.

Application-group switch panel (GroupNextKey / GroupPrevKey)
------------------------------------------------------------

The switch panel now has three modes:

  * FocusNextKey / FocusPrevKey (flat, all windows) -- unchanged,
    default Mod1+Tab / Mod1+Shift+Tab;
  * GroupWindowNextKey / GroupWindowPrevKey (intra-app, same WM_CLASS)
    -- default Mod4+grave / Mod4+Shift+grave;
  * GroupNextKey / GroupPrevKey (app-group, one tile per WM_CLASS) --
    default Mod4+Tab / Mod4+Shift+Tab, new.

The app-group mode shows one tile per application class. Selecting a
tile focuses the most recently used window of that group. After
switching to a group with GroupNextKey, GroupWindowNextKey cycles the
individual windows of the now-focused group.

The live re-trigger loop now recognizes only the key pair that opened
the panel. Pressing another mode's pair while a panel is open closes
it and opens that other mode's switcher.

Upgrade note: GroupNextKey and GroupPrevKey previously controlled the
intra-app switcher (default None). They now control the app-group
switcher, and the intra-app switcher has been renamed to
GroupWindowNextKey / GroupWindowPrevKey. An existing domain with
GroupNextKey = None leaves the app-group switcher unbound until the
stale line is removed or rebound in WPrefs. The intra-app switcher
picks up its new Mod4+grave default automatically. A custom
GroupNextKey binding now drives the app-group switcher. Intra-app
falls back to Mod4+grave.

Letter-key window lookup in the Alt+Tab switch panel
-----------------------------------------------------

While the switch panel is open, pressing a letter key moves the
selection to the next entry whose displayed label starts with that
letter, wrapping at the end of the list. The label is the application
class (`WM_CLASS`) in the app-group switcher and the window title
everywhere else, so in the flat panel and in the intra-app switcher
the letter matches the title. Shift+letter scans backward,
mirroring Shift+Tab. An unmatched letter is ignored, so several
letters can be tried in one cycle. A non-letter key still dismisses
the panel as before.

wmglyph Unicode glyph selector
------------------------------

The new "wmglyph" utility is an emoji-picker-style browser over the
Unicode named-character data, including the algorithmic Hangul
syllable and CJK ideograph ranges expanded by name formula. A search
field and a block dropdown filter a scrollable glyph grid, and
clicking a character copies its UTF-8 to both PRIMARY and CLIPBOARD.
Result sets wider than the grid are paginated with Prev/Next buttons
beside the status bar. Legacy XA_STRING requestors receive Latin-1
when possible and a conversion failure otherwise, so they no longer
paste mojibake. The character and block tables are generated from
UnicodeData.txt and Blocks.txt and committed under the Unicode
License v3, so the build needs no network fetch.

Dynamic workspaces (auto-destroy)
---------------------------------

Empty trailing workspaces are now destroyed automatically when a
window-membership change leaves them at the end of the workspace array.
The new `AutoRemoveEmptyWorkspaces` default (NO) enables the sweep,
and `MinWorkspaceCount` (default 1) sets a floor below which
workspaces are never destroyed. The currently displayed workspace is
never destroyed even if empty, so navigating onto a fresh workspace and
back does not yank the user's view. Only window destruction and
membership moves/removals trigger a sweep (iconifying or hiding a
window does not). The sweep reaps only contiguous empty workspaces at
the end of the array, so the numbering of workspaces still in use
stays stable. Together with the existing `AdvanceToNewWorkspace`
(auto-create) toggle the workspace set now grows and shrinks with use.
The two toggles are independent. A WPrefs control has been added to
the Workspace Navigation panel.

Virtual desktop viewport panning
--------------------------------

A virtual desktop larger than the physical screen is back. The
viewport code now compiles in by default; pass
`--disable-virtual-desktop` to omit it. The feature is opt-in at run
time via the `EnableVirtualDesktop` default (NO). On a floating
(tiling-disabled) workspace, the desktop extends past the screen edges
and is panned by moving every non-sticky window with the window-move
model: a pan subtracts the viewport delta from each window's
frame_x/frame_y and calls XMoveWindow, so the on-screen position is
the world coordinate and no compositor is required.

Panning is driven four ways:

  * keyboard: VirtualEdgeLeftKey / RightKey / UpKey / DownKey snap to
    the nearest window edge in the chosen direction;
  * edge-scroll: 1px InputOnly windows along each head's outer edge
    pan towards the pointer when it dwells there, with resistance and
    attraction thresholds (VirtualEdgeResistance / VirtualEdgeAttraction)
    and configurable scroll speeds;
  * EWMH: _NET_DESKTOP_VIEWPORT ClientMessage requests are honoured when
    they land inside the workspace's finite extent, and the
    _NET_DESKTOP_VIEWPORT / _NET_DESKTOP_GEOMETRY root properties are
    maintained so pagers track the viewport;
  * IPC: `wmakermsg set-viewport X Y` pans programmatically.

Three companion keybinds complement the pan keys: VirtualDesktopHomeKey
resets the camera to (0,0), VirtualDesktopCenterKey pans so the focused
window is screen-centered, and VirtualDesktopPinKey toggles
virtual_stick on the focused window so it stays put on subsequent pans.
EWMH _NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE_{DESKTOP,DOCK,TOOLBAR} windows are
pan-sticky by default, matching their existing dont_move_off handling.

The desktop extent is finite for now: it grows to fit every
non-sticky window's outer frame border (computed from the configured
FrameBorderWidth on each side, not a hardcoded constant) and the
viewport is clamped within [vdesk_x, vdesk_x + vdesk_w - scr_w]. A
workspace whose layout is not WLAYOUT_FLOAT cannot pan — the tiler
would snap windows back. Per-head workspaces and WorkspaceOutputs
output-pinned workspaces leave the feature inert (a per-head guard
lands in a follow-up).

Dynamic window rules
--------------------

WMWindowAttributes now accepts a top-level DynamicRules array whose
entries match windows by regular expression and overlay attributes on
top of the usual per-instance/per-class stack. Each rule combines any
of seven predicates with implicit AND logic:

  MatchClass         (WM_CLASS res_class)
  MatchInstance      (WM_CLASS res_instance)
  MatchTitle         (WM_NAME / _NET_WM_NAME, live-updated)
  MatchRole          (WM_WINDOW_ROLE)
  MatchCommand       (WM_COMMAND, joined)
  MatchMachine       (WM_CLIENT_MACHINE)
  MatchWindowType    (_NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE atom name; matches if any
                      advertised type satisfies the regex)

The first rule (in array order) whose every present predicate matches
is layered on top of the four-level instance.class/instance/class/"*"
precedence, so a dynamic rule wins over even an exact per-app entry.
For example

  DynamicRules = (
    {
      MatchClass = "^Firefox$";
      MatchTitle = ".*YouTube.*";
      Attributes = {
        NoTitlebar = YES;
        KeepOnTop = YES;
      };
    }
  );

drops the titlebar and floats every Firefox window whose title contains
"YouTube". Rules are compiled once when the domain is reloaded.

Because only MatchTitle's source changes on a WM_NAME/_NET_WM_NAME
update, the re-evaluation hook lives inside the existing
wWindowUpdateName() convergence point: when a title change makes a
previously-matching rule stop matching, the attributes it supplied are
reverted to their non-rule baseline (the four-level merge without the
regex layer). A per-window regex_applied_mask tracks which legal-delta
fields the regex layer supplied so reversion does not clobber values
set by a non-rule layer (e.g. an exact per-app entry). Map-time-only
attributes (StartWorkspace, StartMiniaturized, StartHidden,
StartMaximized, AutoTabGroup, StartGeometry, MarkKey) are not
re-evaluated.

Among these, MarkKey tags a window with a vim-like mark label at manage
time, with last-opened-wins semantics: when a new window matches a
MarkKey = "label" rule, any existing holder of that label is unmarked
first, then the newcomer receives it. It works in both rule
shapes that share the Attributes dictionary — a per-app static entry

  xterm.XTerm = { MarkKey = "t"; };

and a DynamicRules entry

  DynamicRules = (
    { MatchClass = "^XTerm$"; Attributes = { MarkKey = "t"; }; }
  );

The value is a three-state string. A non-empty label is stolen globally
and a warning naming the displaced holder and the incoming window is
logged when a steal happens, so misconfigured rules whose class matches
many concurrently-open windows are diagnosable. An empty string
(MarkKey = "") is a local unmark that clears the window's own mark
only; omitting the key leaves the mark untouched. Because the per-window
mark saved in WMState (set interactively with MarkSetKey or carried over
from a prior rule application) is the more specific per-window signal,
a restored mark wins for the incoming window and the rule does not
reassign it; the steal, however, is unconditional with respect to the
existing holder, which is unmarked so the newcomer can take it.

Inside any Match* regex pattern, the sequence $_NAME (where NAME matches
the C-identifier rule [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*) is replaced at reload
time with the value of the environment variable NAME, auto-escaped for
POSIX ERE so the substituted value is treated as a literal substring.
This lets the same rule file stay portable across machines without
hard-coding host-specific values:

  MatchTitle = ".*@$_HOSTNAME:.*";

Unset environment variables substitute to the empty string. $_ with no
following name byte, and $ not followed by _, are passed through
verbatim (the latter is the ERE end-anchor). Substitution does not apply
inside bracket expressions [...] where backslash escapes are not
honoured, nor to Attributes string values (Icon, StartWorkspace,
StartGeometry, the frame color specs) which are not regex.

Per-window frame border overrides
---------------------------------

The seven frame-border settings that previously lived only on the
screen-global WindowMaker domain — FrameBorderWidth, FrameBorderColor,
FrameFocusedBorderColor, FrameSelectedBorderColor, FrameInnerBorderWidth,
FrameInnerBorderColor and FrameInnerFocusedBorderColor — can now be
overridden per window class in WMWindowAttributes. An entry such as

  xterm.XTerm = {
    FrameInnerFocusedBorderColor = green;
    FrameBorderWidth = 3;
  };

gives every xterm a thicker outer border and a green focused inner
border, while every other application keeps the screen-global value.
Color specs are resolved at manage time and fall back to the
screen-global color when no per-window override is set.

StartGeometry attribute
----------------------

A new StartGeometry key in WMWindowAttributes pins a window class to a
fixed initial position and size at map time. The value is a standard
X geometry string of the form WxH+X+Y (e.g. "800x600+0+0"), where W
and H are the requested client width and height and X and Y are the
absolute frame coordinates. Negative X or Y offsets the window from
the right or bottom screen edge. The client's WM_NORMAL_HINTS size
increments are still honoured.

Configurable inner frame border
-------------------------------

A second border can now be drawn inside the outer frame border, between
it and the client window. Four runtime-reloadable defaults control
it: FrameInnerBorderWidth (0 by default, so the look is unchanged unless
raised), FrameInnerBorderColor and FrameInnerFocusedBorderColor (the
unfocused and focused inner-border colors, defaulting to #b5b5b5 and
white), and FrameInnerBorderSides which selects which of the four sides
the inset is painted on. The default is all four sides ("left", "right",
"top", "bottom"), so a config that only sets FrameInnerBorderWidth keeps
the symmetric look; a subset such as ("top", "left") paints only those
strips. An empty list or an unset override also means all sides.

The inset is rendered as four strips painted on the frame window and is
only applied to client-window frames, so menus and docks keep their
existing chrome. FrameInnerBorderSides can also be overridden per window
class in WMWindowAttributes alongside the other frame-border settings.

_NET_FRAME_EXTENTS now reports the inner-border width per side (left,
right, top, bottom) alongside the outer frame border, so clients and
pagers see the true geometry even when the inset is asymmetric. Window
placement, interactive resize, the shade animation, the keyboard move/
resize geometry and internal windows all account for the per-side
inset.

Multiple workspace membership
-----------------------------

A window can now belong to more than one workspace at a time. The window
menu's "Move To" submenu is renamed "Workspaces" and shows a check mark
next to every workspace the window is on, clicking an entry toggles that
membership. The
ToggleWorkspace1Key .. ToggleWorkspace10Key keyboard shortcuts do the same
for the focused window, and `wmakermsg window-workspace XID +N` / `-N` add
or remove a single membership over IPC. MoveToWorkspaceNKey and the plain
`window-workspace XID N` form keep their existing "put it on N and nowhere
else" meaning. A multi-member window is shown as `[Name+]` in the Windows
list and is placed by whichever member workspace's tiling layout is
currently active. The maximum number of workspaces is now 64.

The Inspector's "Initial Workspaces" control is now a multi-select
dropdown that seeds this multi-bit membership at manage time. The
selection is saved as a proplist array under the new "StartWorkspaces"
key (lowest set bit is the primary, rest is OR-ed into the ws_mask). The
old singular "StartWorkspace" key is still read for backward
compatibility. An empty selection means "no opinion".

Per-workspace tiling layouts
----------------------------

Each workspace can now carry an opt-in tiling layout that arranges
every eligible window on it into a fixed pattern. Five layouts are
available: float (the default and exactly today's behaviour, no layout
pass at all), tile (a master column at a configurable width ratio with
the remaining windows stacked to its right), tileh (the same with a
horizontal master row), grid (a near-square grid of equal cells) and
monocle (every window at the full usable area, focused one on top).

The layout, master-window count and master-column ratio are stored per
workspace in WMState and survive a restart. New workspaces pick their
initial layout from DefaultWorkspaceLayout, LayoutMasterCount and
LayoutMasterRatio in the WindowMaker defaults domain. Ten new keyboard
shortcuts (all unbound by default) cycle the layout, swap its axis,
force it back to float, adjust the master count and ratio, toggle the
focused window in and out of the layout, and swap it into the master
slot. The chosen layout name is flashed in the key-chain OSD after a
cycle when that OSD is enabled.

Windows are exempt from tiling when they are transient, omnipresent,
minimised, shaded, fullscreen, non-resizable, kept-on-top or -bottom,
or carry the new NoTiling attribute (settable per class via
WMWindowAttributes and per window via the inspector's "Do not tile"
checkbox). Starting an interactive move or resize on a tiled window
pops it out of the layout in place and reflows the others around it.

The IPC set-layout verb sets a workspace's layout from wmakermsg or
scripts, get_workspaces reports each workspace's layout, master count
and ratio, and the workspace event stream now includes layout changes.

Tiled windows ignore geometry requests from the client itself: a
ConfigureRequest carrying a new position or size is refused and
answered with a configure event reporting the real cell geometry, so
an application that resizes itself (a terminal's resize escape, a
toolkit reflowing at startup) stays inside its cell instead of
escaping the layout or corrupting the geometry restored on untile.
The existing WindowGap option now also spaces the tile, tileh and
grid cells: gaps sit between neighbouring windows and columns while
the outer edges stay flush with the usable area (monocle stays
gap-free), and changing the value re-flows the current workspace.
When minimum sizes or gaps exhaust the available space, windows may
overlap instead of drifting off-screen. A window whose own minimum size
exceeds the usable area still extends beyond it.

Cairo and PangoCairo are now required build dependencies
--------------------------------------------------------

Window titles, menu entries, on-screen popups, help balloons and every
WINGs widget label are now shaped and rendered through PangoCairo onto
cairo-xcb surfaces on the shared xcb connection. The previous optional
--disable-pango path and its raw single-font fallback are removed:
Pango is what provides per-script font fallback, complex-script shaping
and bidirectional layout, so a build without it left CJK and RTL text
broken and there is no reason to keep that configuration. Widget bevels,
the boxed geometry / workspace-name / key-chain popups and the shaped
help balloon are painted through cairo, and under a compositing manager
the non-opaque move/resize outline is a translucent-fill ARGB window.

libwraster gains a small cairo bridge (RImageCairoSurface() and
gradient-pattern helpers) so callers can paint an RImage or a
horizontal/vertical/diagonal gradient straight from a cairo pattern.

configure requires cairo (>= 1.16 with the xcb backend) and pangocairo,
which every maintained distribution ships. Packagers should add
libcairo2-dev / cairo-devel and libpango1.0-dev / pango-devel to the
build dependencies.

Window decorations, dialogs and WPrefs scale with Xft.dpi
---------------------------------------------------------

When the Xft.dpi X resource is set (typically via ~/.Xresources and
xrdb, or by a display manager), Window Maker now derives a UI scale
factor from it (Xft.dpi / 96) and applies it to titlebar and menu
text, the resizebar, the frame border, and every WINGs widget window
including WPrefs, the window inspector, the run dialog, the switch
panel and alert panels. Widget geometry, text and icon pixmaps are
rendered at device resolution, so a 4K display at Xft.dpi 192 gets
crisp 2x decorations and dialogs rather than tiny 96-dpi ones.

To try it without editing ~/.Xresources, override the resource for the
current display:

    echo "Xft.dpi: 144" | xrdb -override -

When Xft.dpi is not set nothing changes: the scale is 1.0 rather than
guessed from the reported physical screen size, which is unreliable.
Changing Xft.dpi at runtime updates the stored scale but existing
windows keep their size until Window Maker is restarted. Dock and
appicon size follows the IconSize preference as before, and the
titlebar button glyphs are fixed-size bitmaps that stay unscaled.

XDG data and cache directories are split from the configuration root
--------------------------------------------------------------------

On the XDG layout (no ~/GNUstep/Defaults and no explicit
WMAKER_USER_ROOT), the per-user tree is now split across the three
XDG basedirs instead of nesting everything under ~/.config/WindowMaker:

    defaults domains  ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/WindowMaker/
    themes, styles,   ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-~/.local/share}/WindowMaker/
    backgrounds,
    autostart, menus
    cached pixmaps,   ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/WindowMaker/
    WPrefs previews

The Defaults/ and Library/WindowMaker/ intermediate directories are
gone on this layout, so the WindowMaker domain sits directly at
~/.config/WindowMaker/WindowMaker and themes at
~/.local/share/WindowMaker/Themes.

An explicit WMAKER_USER_ROOT and an existing ~/GNUstep tree keep the
single-tree Defaults/ + Library/WindowMaker/ layout unchanged, so
existing installs are unaffected. Two additional environment variables
WMAKER_USER_DATA and WMAKER_USER_CACHE are exported alongside
WMAKER_USER_ROOT with the resolved data and cache directories, and the
seeded root menu, PixmapPath and IconPath reference them so the same
templates work on both layouts.

SVG icons are loaded natively
-----------------------------

libwraster now decodes SVG through librsvg (>= 2.46) rendered into a
cairo image surface. The freedesktop icon-theme resolver descends the
hicolor scalable/ directory when no exact-size raster is available, so
applications that ship only a scalable icon get their proper artwork in
the XDG root menu, the run dialog and dock slots filled from a .desktop
file instead of the fallback tile. RLoadImageAtSize() lets callers
request a specific render size for a vector image, and wmsetbg and wmiv
gain SVG through the existing RLoadImage() dispatch. The loader is
optional (configure --disable-svg) and off when librsvg is not found.

Workspace-name popup drawn as an OSD box
----------------------------------------

The workspace name shown on a workspace switch is now drawn in the
same boxed style as the move/resize geometry readout and the
key-chain prefix display, using LargeDisplayFont on the head under
the pointer. The previous shaped floating text with a screenshot
fade-out has been retired along with its per-switch root-window
XGetImage. The corner and edge WorkspaceNameDisplayPosition values
now anchor to the pointer head instead of the whole root window on
a multi-monitor layout so the popup follows the switch to the
output it was triggered on. The visible lifetime is unchanged.

ResizeDisplay = "Line" removed
------------------------------

The technical-drawing style for the interactive resize geometry
readout has been removed. It XOR-drew dimension arrows and numbers
directly on the root window and was the only reason a non-opaque
resize still had to grab the X server on every step. An existing
ResizeDisplay = Line (or MoveDisplay = Line, which was already
non-functional) in a user's WindowMaker domain now warns once and
falls back to the "Center" default. The remaining Corner, Center,
Floating and None values are unchanged and the WPrefs pop-up for
ResizeDisplay no longer lists the Technical drawing-like row.

Window Maker registers with an XSMP session manager
---------------------------------------------------

When started under a session manager that exports SESSION_MANAGER,
Window Maker now opens an XSMP connection so the session manager can
checkpoint and restart it. On a SaveYourself request the window list
is snapshotted in the phase-2 slot after every other client has
answered, so the on-disk WMState reflects the full set of managed
windows at the moment of the checkpoint. The root-menu Exit and Kill
X session entries ask the session manager for an orderly logout when
connected and only fall back to closing clients directly when no
session manager is present. Under a plain startx or xinit session
nothing changes. Pass --disable-xsmp to configure to drop the libSM
and libICE link and keep the previous behaviour unconditionally.

Decorative work pauses while the display is off
-----------------------------------------------

Window Maker now tracks whether any output is actually showing the
framebuffer. While the display is off the urgent-icon bounce and
selection-ants timers disarm and every miniaturize, shade, slide and
workspace-name animation takes the instant path so an idle laptop
reaches zero scheduled wakeups even when a background client raises
the urgency hint. The state is detected natively from RandR (lid
close disconnects the internal output) and from MIT-SCREEN-SAVER
notify (DPMS blank, idle timeout) when the build found
xcb-screensaver, and can be driven by a lid or screensaver hook with
`wmakermsg display-power off|on`.

Two new shell-command hooks fire on the detected transitions:
DisplayOffCommand runs when the display goes dark and
DisplayOnCommand when it comes back, set DisplayOffCommand =
"wmlock" to lock the moment the lid closes. The hooks do not fire
for the IPC verb so a script that itself drives display-power does
not recursively launch the configured locker. A `display_power` IPC
event with state and source is emitted on every transition for
consumers that prefer `wmakermsg -m display_power` over the shell
hook.

Run dialog: XDG completion, popup list, and prefix modes
-------------------------------------------------------

The %A() input dialog used by the "Run..." root-menu entry (now
relabeled "Find...") now also matches the first word against the
Name= of every visible .desktop entry in the XDG_DATA_HOME and
XDG_DATA_DIRS applications directories, in addition to the existing $PATH executable and
filesystem completion. Matching is a case-insensitive substring, so
typing fox<Tab> finds Firefox and cycling with Tab inserts the
application's Exec= line with %f/%U field codes stripped. While
cycling the message line shows [i/N] and the matched application's
Name and Comment. Set RunDialogXDGCompletion = NO to keep the
previous $PATH-only completion. The application list is built on
first use and refreshed automatically when an applications directory
changes, so a freshly installed package is picked up without
restarting the window manager.

When the typed word matches no application or executable the same
dialog now searches documents under the XDG Documents, Downloads
and Desktop directories and any extra roots listed in the new
RunDialogDocumentPaths array, picking a match inserts the file's
default MIME handler with the quoted path as argument (or xdg-open
when no handler is known). A leading ? forces document mode
regardless, so ?repo<Tab> finds Annual report.pdf even if a repo
binary exists. RunDialogDocumentSearch = NO disables this,
RunDialogDocumentDepth (default 3) and RunDialogDocumentMax
(default 5000) bound the index walk.

The RunDialogMatching default selects the matching algorithm for
application, executable and document names. The default substring
is the case-insensitive contains match described above, fuzzy is a
greedy subsequence matcher with positional bonuses so loff<Tab>
finds LibreOffice, and prefix restores the pre-series anchored
match for users who dislike the new ranking. Filesystem path
completion after a / stays prefix-only in every mode.

When Tab finds two or more candidates a borderless list pops up
flush under the dialog showing each match's name and the command
that would be inserted. Up/Down or Tab/Shift+Tab move through it
and the text field tracks the selection, typing narrows the list
live, a single click applies a row and a double click runs it,
Escape dismisses the popup and restores the typed text without
closing the dialog. RunDialogPopupList = NO falls back to the
[i/N] label cycle only and RunDialogPopupRows (default 8, range
3 to 20) caps the visible height. Each row carries an icon in a
left gutter, the application's Icon= resolved through the hicolor
fallback theme for XDG entries, the file's MIME-type icon for
documents and the generic application-x-executable for $PATH
binaries. Icons are loaded lazily on first draw and cached for
the lifetime of the dialog so scrolling stays cheap.

Applications and documents launched from the popup are recorded
in $XDG_STATE_HOME/WindowMaker/RunFrecency and a hits-decayed-by-age
bonus is folded into the match score so frequently-used entries
rise to the top of their rank band over time. One-hit entries
older than 90 days are pruned on write so the file stays small.

When the popup selection is on a document and at least two
visible applications declare its MIME type in their MimeType=
list, the Right arrow drills into an open-with sub-list of those
handlers (each row showing the document name and the application
it would open with) and Left or Escape returns to the outer
document list at the same row.

A leading = in the first word evaluates an arithmetic expression
(operators + - * / % ^, parentheses, functions sin cos tan asin acos
atan atan2 sqrt cbrt exp log log2 log10 pow fabs floor ceil round hypot
fmin fmax, constants pi e tau); the result appears in the completion
popup as you type and pressing Return dismisses the dialog without
running anything, so =2+3 shows 5 and closes. RunDialogCalculator
(default YES) gates this mode, and = expressions are recorded in a
separate Calc history file so Up/Down recalls both commands and
expressions.

A leading # searches managed windows by title, WM_CLASS and
WM_INSTANCE across all workspaces (## limits the search to the
current workspace) and accepting a row focuses that window.
RunDialogWindowSwitcher (default YES) gates this mode.

A leading @ jumps to a matching workspace by 1-based index or name;
@ws#needle lists windows on the workspaces matching ws and accepting
a row focuses that window. RunDialogWorkspaceJumper (default YES)
gates the bare @ jump; @ws#needle is gated by RunDialogWindowSwitcher.

Drag a file onto the dock to create a launcher
-----------------------------------------------

Dropping a single file from any XDND-capable file manager onto the
main dock tile (or the clip tile) now creates a docked launcher in the
next free slot, the application does not need to be running first.
Three kinds of drop are recognised: a GNUstep .app bundle directory
(the bundle's Info-gnustep.plist supplies the executable, the
WM_CLASS instance, the icon, ApplicationName and
ApplicationDescription), a freedesktop .desktop file (Exec,
StartupWMClass, Name, Comment and Icon are read, %f/%U-style field
codes are stripped from the command, and a bare Icon name is resolved
through the hicolor fallback theme), and a plain executable file (the
file path becomes the command, its basename the WM_CLASS).  Anything
else is ignored.  The existing per-tile DropCommand behaviour on the
other dock slots is unchanged.

Docked Application Settings panel: name and description
--------------------------------------------------------

The Settings panel for a docked launcher (right-click -> Settings) now
shows the application name in bold beside the icon preview, with the
description in a smaller font underneath.  Both come from a dropped
.desktop or .app bundle and feed the balloon help shown when the
pointer hovers over the dock tile, so a docked Firefox reads "Firefox
Web Browser / Browse the World Wide Web" instead of "firefox /
(firefox %u)".  The icon preview itself is now a click target for the
icon chooser, the existing Icon Image field and Browse button are kept
for keyboard-only use.  The name and description are saved per
launcher in WMState as DisplayName and Comment.

Window tab grouping
-------------------

Several managed windows can now share one on-screen frame slot. Only
the active member of a group is mapped, the others stay managed but
unmapped (and publish _NET_WM_STATE_HIDDEN so a taskbar can still list
them and clicking one switches to that tab). All members are kept the
same size and follow the active member through move, resize, maximize,
shade, miniaturize, workspace change and application hide as one unit.

Four new keybindings drive it: GroupTabAttachKey attaches the focused
window to the previous suitable window on the workspace,
GroupTabDetachKey peels it off again at a small offset, and
GroupTabNextKey / GroupTabPrevKey cycle the visible member without
changing the group's stacking position. The window menu gains
"Attach as Tab..." (a pointer-pick mode, click the target window or
Escape to cancel), "Detach Tab" and "Close Group". Shift+click on the
close button closes every member of the group, a plain click closes
only the active one and reveals the next tab. A window is only
accepted into a group when it has a titlebar, is movable and
resizable, is normal-level, and is not fullscreen, miniaturized or
hidden.

By default a grouped titlebar is drawn as one segment per member, each
with its own title and (when ShowIconInTitlebar is on) mini-icon.
Button1 on an inactive segment switches to that tab, Ctrl+Button1
closes that member, Button3 opens the window menu for that member, and
the wheel cycles tabs. Button2 on any segment, or Button1-dragging an
inactive segment past the move threshold, picks that member up as a
draggable outline that can be dropped on another titlebar to attach,
on its own group to reorder, or anywhere else to tear it off. Tab
groups survive a Window Maker restart and are reconstructed from a
saved session with the same order and active member.

New defaults: TabsInTitlebar (YES) selects the segmented titlebar
renderer, NO falls back to a plain "[N/M] title" prefix.
ShowAllTabsInSwitchPanel (YES) lists every member individually in
Alt-Tab and the Windows menu, NO collapses each group to its active
member. TabAttachArea (Titlebar) limits drag-to-group drops to the
target's titlebar, Window accepts the whole frame. TabDragModifier
(None) names a modifier that, held with Button1 on any segment
including the active one, starts a tab drag for mice without a
middle button. The keybindings, the two boolean defaults and
TabAttachArea are exposed in WPrefs. A NoTabbing per-window attribute
(Inspector > Advanced) refuses a window as either the dragged tab or
the drop target.

Themes can style inactive tab segments distinctly with
TabInactiveTitleBack (texture) and TabInactiveTitleColor (text
colour). When left at their empty defaults the renderer borrows
PTitleBack and PTitleColor exactly as before so existing themes
are unchanged.

Dropping a file on a GNUstep dock icon passes it as a file
-----------------------------------------------------------

When a file is dragged from a file manager and dropped on a docked
application whose WM_CLASS is "GNUstep", the dropped path(s) are now
prefixed with a single -GSFilePath flag before the expanded %d
(drag-drop) or %s (paste/selection) command token, so GNUstep's
NSApplication parses them as files to open. The existing quoted
file list produced by the XDND selection transfer is reused
unchanged, so multiple drops expand to
app -GSFilePath "path1" "path2" and a non-GNUstep target still gets
the bare quoted list with no prefix. The flag is set per-drop from the
target icon's WM_CLASS and cleared right after execCommand returns,
so it never leaks into a subsequent unrelated launch.

Configurable titlebar button layout
------------------------------------

The titlebar buttons are no longer fixed to "miniaturize on the left,
close on the right". Two new array-typed defaults, TitlebarLeft and
TitlebarRight, list which buttons appear in each cluster and in what
order. Recognised role names are Miniaturize, Close, Maximize, Icon,
and (when built with --enable-modelock) Language. The default
"(Miniaturize) / (Close)" keeps the historical look unchanged. A
macOS-style "(Close, Miniaturize, Maximize) / ()" cluster on the left
is now possible, as is the OPENSTEP look with the application icon in
the titlebar via "(Icon) / (Miniaturize, Close)". The position of
Icon within an array has no visual effect (it always renders adjacent
to the title text under NewStyle = new because the titlebar surface
is shrunk between the button cores). The keys are read once at
startup. With the new layout freedom a long centred title could
overlap a one-sided button cluster, so centred titles are now
clamped to the free span between the left and right offsets, this
also fixes the existing modelock case where a long title overlapped
the language button.

Six new defaults let a style replace the compiled-in titlebar button
glyphs with image files. The Close role draws one of three images
depending on client state and the Maximize role draws one of two
depending on window state, so there is one key per drawn variant
rather than per role:

  CloseButtonPixmap        Close button, normal case
  BrokenCloseButtonPixmap  Close button while the client has flagged
                           the document as edited (the dotted X)
  KillButtonPixmap         Close button when the client does not
                           support WM_DELETE_WINDOW so clicking it
                           kills the process
  MiniaturizeButtonPixmap  Miniaturize button
  MaximizeButtonPixmap     Maximize button while not maximized
  RestoreButtonPixmap      Maximize button while maximized

Each is a filename resolved against PixmapPath (any wrlib-supported
format), an empty value or a load failure keeps the built-in glyph
for that slot, so a style can override any subset and the rest fall
back. Loaded images are blitted as-is so
their own colours survive instead of being stencilled in the title
text colour, the existing convention that an image at least twice as
wide as it is tall holds a second pressed-state picture in its right
half still applies. The keys are read once at startup like NewStyle
and TitlebarLeft. Three sample pixmaps button_close.xpm,
button_miniaturize.xpm and button_maximize.xpm (red, amber and green
circles) are installed in the shared Pixmaps directory so a style can
reference them by bare filename, pairing them with NewStyle = flat
and TitlebarLeft = (Close, Miniaturize, Maximize) gives a
traffic-light cluster on a flat titlebar.

NewStyle = flat is a fourth titlebar style that uses the same
geometry as NewStyle = new (button cores are full-height children of
the frame and the title surface is shrunk between them) but draws no
relief: the per-button raised bevel and the titlebar bevel are
skipped so the title texture runs unbroken across the button cluster
and a theme can ship round button pixmaps that sit flush on it. With
the default monochrome glyphs the press feedback is the glyph
darkening and a one-pixel offset, the white pressed-button box that
NewStyle = new draws is not used.

Maximize is a new button role: a plain click toggles full
horizontal+vertical maximization, a Control-click toggles
fullscreen (same modifier convention as Control-click on Close to
kill and on Miniaturize to hide the application). The button glyph
flips to a "restore" form while the window is maximized. The button
is hidden on non-resizable windows.

Fullscreen window menu entry and key binding
---------------------------------------------

The window menu now has a Fullscreen / Exit Fullscreen entry, and a
new ToggleFullscreenKey binding (default "None") does the same from
the keyboard. This gives a way back out of fullscreen for clients
that do not speak _NET_WM_STATE themselves once the titlebar is gone.

The unrelated DbClickFullScreen option, which despite its name made a
titlebar double-click maximize the window rather than shade it, has
been renamed to DbClickMaximize. The old key name is not read.

wmlock screen locker
--------------------

A native screen locker is now installed as wmlock. It covers every
X screen with a grayscaled, stack-blurred snapshot of the desktop
and shows a WINGs password prompt reparented inside that cover.
The keyboard and pointer grabs are taken on the cover and held for
the entire lifetime of the lock, the prompt is keyboard only, so
no other client can receive input or steal focus while it is up.
Authentication uses the "wmlock" PAM service (a one-line file
including the login auth stack is installed to /etc/pam.d so
fingerprint or token modules can be added without touching the
system login configuration), or auth_userokay(3) on OpenBSD. On
Linux it makes a best-effort attempt to lower its OOM score, mlock
the password buffer, and lock VT switching, all of which fail
harmlessly without the matching capability. wmlock leaves DPMS
alone, pair it with xset or xss-lock if blanking is wanted. The
build is enabled automatically when PAM or bsd_auth headers are
found, override with --disable-wmlock.

A new LockScreenCommand string default (default "wmlock") names the
locker to run, and a new LockScreenKey binding (default "None")
runs it. The shipped root menu templates now use wmlock for their
Lock entry instead of the long-unmaintained xlock.

Unix-socket control channel and the new wmakermsg tool
-------------------------------------------------------

Window Maker now listens on a per-screen Unix-domain socket and
exposes a small request/reply protocol with an event subscription
stream. The socket is created under $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/wmaker (or a
per-user directory under /tmp when XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is unset), its
path is exported to every program launched from the dock or root
menu as WMAKER_IPC_SOCKET, and "wmaker --get-socketpath" prints it
for scripts that are not children. The socket directory is created
mode 0700, the socket itself mode 0600, and the directory's owner
and mode are verified before binding so a pre-planted /tmp entry of
the same name is refused.

The new "wmakermsg" tool is the command-line client. The verbs cover
what the root menu and the existing signal handlers already do
(reconfigure, restart, exit, kill-session, workspace next/prev/last/N,
hide-application, hide-others, show-all, arrange-icons, info), plus
typed get_version/get_workspaces/get_windows queries and a send_tick
synchronisation primitive for test drivers. "wmakermsg -m EVENT..."
subscribes and prints one event per line for workspace, window,
shutdown, child_exit, dialog and tick events. Exit status follows
the usual convention: 0 success, 1 transport failure, 2 command
rejected. The wire format is documented in doc/wmaker-ipc.txt and
the client-side helpers are in the installed <WINGs/WIPC.h>.

WPrefs and setstyle now use this socket to ask Window Maker to
re-read its configuration, falling back to the old root-window
ClientMessage only when the socket is unreachable. The old
_WINDOWMAKER_COMMAND ClientMessage receiver is kept for one release
with a deprecation warning and will be removed in the release after.

The wmlib client library (libWMaker, WMaker.h, wmlib.pc) has been
removed. Its only command transport was the deprecated root-window
ClientMessage, the menu and per-window attribute hints it wrapped
are plain X properties that GNUstep and other toolkits set directly
without it, and the format-32 attribute writer in it had the same
LP64 unit-size hazard as the property reads that were tightened in
the previous release. Programs that used wmlib for "Reconfigure"
should call "wmakermsg reconfigure" or wIPCCommand() instead, and
programs that used WMSetWindowAttributes() or the WMMenu* functions
should set the _GNUSTEP_WM_ATTR or _WINDOWMAKER_MENU property on
their own toplevel directly.


wmclipd clipboard daemon
------------------------

The new "wmclipd" utility runs as a small X11 clipboard helper.  With
--clipboard it monitors the CLIPBOARD and PRIMARY selections via the
XFixes extension and re-owns them when the original application exits,
so a Ctrl+C copy from a closed window can still be pasted, and image
data placed only on PRIMARY is mirrored to CLIPBOARD when no clipboard
owner exists.  With --screenshots it subscribes to the new IPC
"screenshot" event and offers each saved screenshot on CLIPBOARD as
image/png (or image/jpeg), text/uri-list and the file path, so a
screenshot taken with the ScreenshotKey can be pasted straight into
another application.  Either or both modes can be enabled, see
wmclipd(1).  XFixes is now an auto-detected build dependency,
--without-xfixes disables it and skips building wmclipd.

wmiv gains Ctrl+V to paste an image/png or image/jpeg from CLIPBOARD,
appending it to the image list as a temporary file.

wmclipd also keeps a bounded in-memory history ring of the last N
captured CLIPBOARD/PRIMARY entries (default 10, --history 0 disables)
and offers it through a popup bound to Super+v by the new
ClipboardKey/ClipboardCommand defaults pair. The popup lists entries
with a bold label and dim detail line, supports v/arrows/j/k/Home/End
navigation, p to pin (exempt from eviction and clear-all), Delete to
remove one entry, Shift+Delete to clear non-pinned entries, and
Return/click or release-modifier to commit Alt+Tab style.


Startup-notification launcher and monitor
------------------------------------------

When libstartup-notification-1.0 is present at build time, Window
Maker now broadcasts a startup-notification sequence for every dock,
clip, root-menu and relaunch action, exporting DESKTOP_STARTUP_ID to
the spawned process and showing a busy root cursor until the
application maps a window or 20 seconds pass.  On the receiving side
it reads _NET_STARTUP_ID on newly managed windows and matches it back
to the pending sequence, so the timestamp of the launching click
becomes the window's effective _NET_WM_USER_TIME: if you kept typing
elsewhere while a slow application was starting it now opens in the
background with _NET_WM_STATE_DEMANDS_ATTENTION instead of stealing
focus, and it lands on the workspace it was launched from rather than
wherever you have since moved.  The library is auto-detected,
--without-startup-notification disables both halves.


Per-head independent workspaces
-------------------------------

Setting "PerHeadWorkspaces = YES" in ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker
lets each monitor keep its own current workspace. The workspace
namespace stays global, but each head independently chooses which
workspace from that set is visible, so switching workspace on one
monitor leaves windows on the others where they are.
_NET_CURRENT_DESKTOP follows the focused head, so toolkits that gate
drag-and-drop visibility on it (notably Chromium-based browsers) see
the workspace under the pointer regardless of which monitor it is on.

The optional "WorkspaceOutputs" dictionary pins workspaces to RandR
output names. List connected outputs with "xrandr --listmonitors" and
key by 1-based workspace number (or workspace name) with a single
output name or an array:

    PerHeadWorkspaces = YES;
    WorkspaceOutputs = {
      "1" = "DP-1";
      "2" = "DP-2";
      "3" = ("DP-2", "HDMI-1");
    };

Unpinned workspaces are allowed on every head, a workspace whose pinned
outputs are all absent is allowed on no head until one reappears, and
on a setup where heads have no RandR output name a "#N" head-index key
can be used instead. Next/Prev workspace and the move-window-to-next/
prev keybindings step only through workspaces allowed on the active
head, and sending a window to a workspace pinned to another monitor
carries the window there. Per-head state is saved to WMState by output
name (with a "#N" index fallback for unnamed heads) and remapped on
RandR hotplug, so a monitor keeps its workspace across reconnects and
head reorders. A toggle for the option is in WPrefs, the
WorkspaceOutputs dictionary is text-only for now, and the option is
applied at startup only. The workspace map overlay does not yet show
per-head state. The default is NO.

Separately, the new "AutoFocusAcrossHeads" option (default YES,
matching previous behaviour) restricts auto-focus of a newly mapped
window to the head the pointer is on when set to NO, and a non-pager
_NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW request for a window on another head is then
refused with _NET_WM_STATE_DEMANDS_ATTENTION instead of stealing
focus across monitors.


Extended Window Manager Hints (EWMH) coverage
---------------------------------------------

Every window-manager hint in the EWMH 1.5 specification that is not
compositor-specific is now handled, and everything advertised in
_NET_SUPPORTED is exercised by an automated conformance test under a
two-head nested X server. New since the last release:

- _NET_WM_PING: closing a window now sends a ping alongside
  WM_DELETE_WINDOW. If the client does not echo it within five seconds
  an "Application Not Responding" dialog offers Force Quit (SIGTERM,
  escalating to SIGKILL, on the _NET_WM_PID cached at map time when
  WM_CLIENT_MACHINE matches the local host, otherwise XKillClient) or
  Wait.
- _NET_WM_USER_TIME / _NET_WM_USER_TIME_WINDOW focus-stealing
  prevention. A newly mapped window whose user time is 0 or older than
  the most recent user activity is not focused automatically and is
  marked _NET_WM_STATE_DEMANDS_ATTENTION instead. _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW
  requests from applications are refused on the same basis. A
  transient or _NET_WM_STATE_MODAL dialog of the focused window's
  application, and any window mapping into a higher stacking layer
  than the current focus, are exempt from the timestamp check, and
  finishing an interactive move or resize counts as user activity so
  a window mapping right after does not take focus from the window
  just dropped.
- _NET_WM_STATE_DEMANDS_ATTENTION drives the same urgent appicon
  bounce as the ICCCM UrgencyHint and is reflected in _NET_WM_STATE
  for pagers, and _NET_WM_STATE_MODAL on a transient redirects focus
  to the dialog when its parent is clicked or selected in the switch
  panel.
- _NET_DESKTOP_LAYOUT: the new LeftWorkspaceKey, RightWorkspaceKey,
  UpWorkspaceKey and DownWorkspaceKey bindings (and their MoveTo...
  variants) and the workspace map overlay step through whatever grid a
  pager has set. With no pager the layout defaults to a single row.
  All eight bindings default to None.
- _NET_WM_SYNC_REQUEST: opaque interactive resizes are paced to the
  client's redraw when the X Synchronization extension is available
  and the client exposes _NET_WM_SYNC_REQUEST_COUNTER. Disable with
  --disable-xsync at configure time.
- _NET_MOVERESIZE_WINDOW (so wmctrl -e and pager-driven moves work),
  _NET_RESTACK_WINDOW, _NET_REQUEST_FRAME_EXTENTS,
  _NET_WM_VISIBLE_NAME and _NET_WM_VISIBLE_ICON_NAME, and
  _NET_WM_FULL_PLACEMENT is advertised so toolkits that honour it let
  the window manager place new windows. _NET_WM_ACTION_ABOVE/_BELOW
  are listed in _NET_WM_ALLOWED_ACTIONS, and _NET_CLOSE_WINDOW is
  listed in _NET_SUPPORTED.
- _NET_WM_STRUT_PARTIAL start/end fields are honoured per head so a
  panel reserving only part of one monitor edge no longer shrinks the
  usable area on every other monitor, _NET_WORKAREA is rewritten
  whenever a strut changes, _NET_DESKTOP_VIEWPORT and
  _NET_CLIENT_LIST_STACKING are kept current, _NET_DESKTOP_NAMES
  applies to the first workspace as well as the rest, and a
  _NET_WM_ICON change after the window has mapped is reflected in the
  application icon and the switch panel.


RandR support now requires version 1.5
--------------------------------------

The multi-monitor head list is now read via the RandR 1.5 GetMonitors
request, which returns each logical monitor's name, geometry and
primary flag in one round trip and coalesces mirrored outputs
server-side. The runtime check and the libXrandr configure probe both
require RandR 1.5 (xorg-server 1.18 / libXrandr 1.5.0, both 2015).
The per-output/per-CRTC resources walk is now confined to the
HotplugMonitor "xrandr --auto" path, so head detection at startup and
on a passive layout change no longer issues a request per output.


xcb and xkbcommon are now required dependencies
------------------------------------------------

libWINGs now issues all window-property and ICCCM hint requests over
xcb, sharing the existing X connection via x11-xcb, and the window
manager loads the keyboard map through libxkbcommon so it tracks
layout, level and modifier changes from XKB events instead of the
libX11 cache. The pkg-config modules xcb, xcb-icccm, x11-xcb,
xcb-randr, xcb-shape, xcb-sync, xcb-xkb, xkbcommon and xkbcommon-x11
must be present at build time, and there is no configure switch to
disable this. Per-window property
reads at startup and when adopting a new client are batched into a
single server round-trip on top of this, so starting Window Maker (or
taking over with --replace) with many existing clients is noticeably
quicker, especially over a remote or high-latency display.

The Window Maker process now also reads the X event stream over xcb
instead of through Xlib. Text fields owned by the window manager
itself (the window-inspector specification fields, the icon-chooser
file path, the workspace-rename and crash-dialog inputs) talk to a
running fcitx5 or ibus server through xcb-imdkit when that library
is present at build time, and fall back to a local xkbcommon compose
table for dead keys and Compose sequences otherwise. xcb-imdkit is
an optional dependency, --without-xcb-imdkit forces the compose-only
path. WPrefs.app, wmagnify and wmiv are separate processes and keep
the original libX11 input-method client unchanged.


libev is now a required dependency
----------------------------------

The WINGs/WUtil event loop (timers, idle handlers, and file-descriptor
input handlers) is now driven by libev. libev picks the best available
backend at runtime, so the loop uses epoll on Linux and kqueue on the
BSDs with no per-platform code path. Timer handlers are scheduled on a
monotonic clock, so wall-clock jumps from NTP corrections or suspend
and resume no longer fire balloon, auto-raise, double-click, dock
auto-collapse and defaults-reload timers early or stall them. The
libev development package must be present at build time, there is no
configure switch to disable this.

For libWUtil consumers: WHandleEvents() now blocks until the next
timer is due when only timers are registered, instead of returning
immediately. With nothing registered it still returns at once. The
internal W_CheckTimerHandlers()/W_CheckIdleHandlers() entry points are
gone from WINGsP.h.


Indexed-colour displays are no longer supported
-----------------------------------------------

The wraster library now requires a TrueColor visual and fails
RCreateContext() with the new RERR_BADVISUALCLASS code on PseudoColor,
StaticColor, GrayScale or StaticGray screens. The PseudoColor colour
cube allocator, the X standard-colormap lookup and the Floyd-Steinberg
dithering paths have been removed, libwraster no longer links libXmu,
and its soname moves from 6 to 9. The ColormapSize and DisableDithering
defaults keys, the corresponding "Dithering colormap for 8bpp" panel in
WPrefs, and the WRASTER_COLOR_RESOLUTION environment variable are gone.
wmsetbg still accepts -c, -d and -m for backward compatibility with old
scripts and themes but they have no effect and are no longer documented.
15 and 16-bit TrueColor visuals continue to work via the masked
best-match path, without error diffusion.


Typed configuration parsing
---------------------------

The WindowMaker defaults domain is now parsed once into a typed
configuration model and diffed against the previously applied state on
reload, instead of being string-compared and re-converted key by key.
User configuration files keep the same property-list format and keys,
but a few values are validated more strictly:

- integer options (RaiseDelay, EdgeResistance, the various *Delay and
  *Size settings, etc.) no longer accept trailing text after the
  number, a value such as "100ms" now warns and falls back to the
  built-in default instead of silently reading 100,
- IconSize must be at least 1, DoubleClickTime at least 1, and
  RaiseDelay/ClipAutoraiseDelay/ClipAutolowerDelay/ClipAutoexpandDelay/
  ClipAutocollapseDelay must be non-negative.

WindowMaker/Defaults/WindowMaker now lists every recognised key with
its built-in default and is kept in sync with the schema by make check.


Hot corners fire under client windows, fewer idle wake-ups
----------------------------------------------------------

Hot corners are now driven by small InputOnly windows kept above all
clients instead of root-window motion events, so a corner fires even
when a maximised or fullscreen window covers it. The trade-off is that
the HotCornerEdge-sized square in each corner does not pass button
clicks through to the client visually underneath while HotCorners is
on. Separately, root-window pointer motion is no longer tracked at all
unless ScrollableMenus is enabled and a menu is actually mapped, so
with the default configuration the event loop stays asleep while the
pointer crosses an empty desktop. Changes written to the defaults
files are also now noticed within about 100 ms even when there is no
X activity, where previously they could wait until the next X event
arrived.


Swallow child window attribute
------------------------------

A new "SwallowChild" per-window attribute makes a window hide itself
when a foreground child process maps its own window, placing the child
at the parent's position and size. When the child closes, the parent
reappears in place. This lets a graphical program launched from a
terminal replace the terminal window until it exits.

The attribute can be set from the window inspector's "Attributes" page
as "Swallow child window", or by adding SwallowChild = YES to the
window's entry in ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WMWindowAttributes (NO by
default). Children started in the background (in a separate process
group) are never swallowed.


GNUstep applications drive their own Hide via _GNUSTEP_WM_HIDE_APP
------------------------------------------------------------------

When a window lists _GNUSTEP_WM_HIDE_APP in WM_PROTOCOLS, the Hide
action from the titlebar miniaturize button (right click or Ctrl+left
click), the HideKey binding, the window menu, and the appicon and dock
menus now sends that protocol message instead of unmapping the
application's windows from the window-manager side. This lets the
application post its applicationWillHide and applicationDidHide
notifications and keep NSApplication.isHidden in step. Applications
that do not advertise the protocol are unaffected. Right-clicking the
miniaturize button on a GNUstep window also now hides as documented,
where previously it sent _GNUSTEP_WM_MINIATURIZE_WINDOW because the
modifier-state test ran before the button-number test.


WMWindowAttributes keys are case-sensitive
------------------------------------------

The WMWindowAttributes domain is now read with case-sensitive keys, so
an entry for the instance "xterm" and another for the class "XTerm" are
kept distinct instead of one silently overwriting the other when the
file is loaded. WM_CLASS strings have always been case-sensitive in
X11, only the file load and global/user merge collapsed them.


Lower X server pixmap usage
---------------------------

Rendered titlebar, resizebar and menu item textures are now shared
between windows and menus of the same width instead of each holding
its own copy on the X server. With several same-width windows open
this roughly halves the pixmap memory Window Maker holds on the
server.


UTF-8 in saved property lists
-----------------------------

Strings written by WMGetPropListDescription() (and therefore every
saved defaults domain, theme and style file) now keep valid UTF-8
characters as-is instead of expanding each byte into a \nnn octal
escape. Invalid byte sequences are still escaped, and existing files
with octal escapes continue to load unchanged.


Back-to-back defaults writes are all picked up
----------------------------------------------

Several wdwrite or setstyle calls issued within the same wall-clock
second are now each applied. The runtime reload previously compared the
defaults file's modification time at one-second resolution, so once the
first write of a burst had been read the rest were treated as unchanged
until something else touched the file in a later second. The comparison
now uses the nanosecond-resolution st_mtim.


WPrefs writes "Smooth font edges" to WMGLOBAL
---------------------------------------------

The "Smooth font edges" checkbox in the Expert panel now reads and
writes AntialiasedText in the user's WMGLOBAL, which is where WINGs
actually looks for it. Previously WPrefs wrote it to the WindowMaker
defaults domain, so toggling the option had no effect on font
rendering.


wxpaste -s reads the named selection again
------------------------------------------

wxpaste -s NAME requested the conversion but never waited for the
owner to reply, so it always fell back to the cut buffer. It now
waits up to one second for the SelectionNotify and writes exactly
the number of bytes the owner returned rather than relying on a
NUL terminator.


Build and configure changes
---------------------------

--with-libs-from and --with-incs-from now also reach the configure-time
feature probes (previously they only affected the substituted build
flags), so optional libraries installed under a non-default prefix are
detected when those options are passed.

configure now requires a working O_NOFOLLOW (POSIX.1-2008) instead of
silently defining it to 0; the safe-copy path in libWUtil depends on it.

--with-libarchive accepts and validates auto|yes|no, and
--with-menu-textdomain rejects values containing characters outside
[A-Za-z0-9_.-].

Unbounded sprintf/strcpy/strcat/strncpy/strncat call sites across the
tree have been replaced with snprintf, wstrlcpy/wstrlcat or length-known
memcpy; no user-visible behaviour change is intended.


Non-opaque move and resize outline, EdgeResistance during resize
-----------------------------------------------------------------

The frame outline shown during a non-opaque move or resize and the
half-screen snap target preview are now drawn with a shaped
override-redirect window instead of an XOR rubber-band on the root.
The display is no longer frozen while the outline is up, so other
clients keep repainting under it, the floating geometry box no
longer leaves a trail, and other applications stay responsive
through a long drag. The outline is a 1-pixel white line between
1-pixel black edges so it reads on both light and dark content.

EdgeResistance and Attraction now also apply to interactive resize.
Growing a window past the screen or strut edge sticks at the
boundary the same way moving past it does, and with Attraction
enabled the dragged edge snaps to the nearest edge of any visible
neighbour it overlaps, both abutting the facing edge and aligning
with the same-side edge. The raw size keeps tracking the pointer so
the snap releases once the drag passes the resistance distance, and
the client's size-hint increments are applied after the snap so a
terminal still rounds to whole character cells.


wmsetbg per-output, mode and color flags
---------------------------------------

wmsetbg now supports per-output wallpaper assignment via --output
<name>, a --mode {tile,center,scale,fit,fill,solid_color} selector
(-m short form), --color / -c for solid backgrounds, and
-X / --stretch-all to stretch one image across all heads. Head
geometry and names are queried via RandR 1.5 GetMonitors with a
XINERAMA fallback. The old -b and --back-color aliases are kept as
deprecated.


Wallpaper-derived tonal palette (wmsetbg --dynamic-color)
--------------------------------------------------------

wrlib gains a bounded median-cut palette extractor
(RExtractPalette) that derives a small set of swatches from the
loaded wallpaper. wmsetbg --dynamic-color[=Auto|Light|Dark] tints
every UI role from the seed hue at a fixed tone:
surface fills use low saturation so a vivid wallpaper does not
produce screaming titlebars, while the highlight accent uses full
seed saturation. Monochrome wallpapers produce neutral grays. The
derived palette is persisted via wdwrite so it survives a restart.


EnforceIconMargin enabled by default
-----------------------------------

Appicons now render at 75% of the tile size by default, leaving room
around the icon so it does not touch the tile border. The
EnforceIconMargin default is now YES; set it to NO to restore the
old edge-to-edge icon filling.


Miniaturize other applications' windows
---------------------------------------

New `MiniaturizeOthersKey` (default `None`) miniaturizes every window
of other applications on the current workspace, leaving the focused
application's windows in place. It mirrors `MiniaturizeAllKey` but
skips the focused app's windows. Configurable in WPrefs as
"Miniaturize other windows".

`MinimizeAllKey` is renamed `MiniaturizeAllKey` for consistency with
the `Miniaturize*` family. No key-name migration exists, so an
existing config that sets `MinimizeAllKey` falls back to the default
`None`.


Pointer follows focus / monitor
-------------------------------

Two independent options:

"PointerFollowsFocus" (default NO, opt-in) — when keyboard focus
changes (Alt-Tab cycling, the window switcher, the directional focus
keys, keyboard tab switching, a workspace switch, or a new window
taking focus via "Automatically focus new windows"), the mouse
pointer is moved to the center of the window that received focus, so
the pointer stays with the active window. When the focused window
closes, hides or iconifies, focus now goes to the window under the
pointer (previously the most-recently-used window was chosen), and
the pointer moves to that window. A closed focused transient hands
focus back to the window it is transient for. With the pointer over
the empty desktop under ClickToFocus, focus otherwise drops to the
root. Restoring windows from miniwindows or dock icons also moves the
pointer.

"PointerFollowsMonitor" (default YES, preserving existing behaviour)
— when you switch to an empty workspace or to another monitor, the
mouse pointer is moved to the center of that monitor or of the head
the pointer is on, so the pointer follows your navigation even when
there is no window to focus. The YES default keeps the monitor-switch
keyboard shortcut's behaviour of warping the pointer to the next
monitor's center (cycling between heads on single-screen multi-head
setups). That warp was previously unconditional and can now be turned
off by setting the option to NO. YES also enables the new
empty-workspace warp.

Enable/disable "PointerFollowsFocus" in WPrefs.app under Window Focus
Preferences. Both options can also be set to "YES" or "NO" in
~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker. "PointerFollowsMonitor" has no WPrefs
UI and must be edited there directly.

Neither option moves the pointer when focus follows the mouse
(Auto / sloppy focus mode) and you focus a window by moving the
pointer into it. The warps only apply to explicit, non-pointer
focus changes. With PointerFollowsFocus enabled, honored window-
activation requests from applications (e.g. pagers and taskbars) can
move the pointer, since they change focus to that window.


-- 0.97.0

XDG-based user configuration directory
--------------------------------------

For new users (no existing ~/GNUstep/Defaults directory), the per-user
configuration root is now ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME}/WindowMaker, defaulting to
~/.config/WindowMaker, instead of the visible ~/GNUstep directory. The
internal layout (Defaults/, Library/WindowMaker/, Library/Icons/) is
unchanged.

Existing installs are unaffected: when ~/GNUstep/Defaults is present it is
used as before, and the WMAKER_USER_ROOT environment variable still takes
precedence over both. WMAKER_USER_ROOT is now also exported to child
processes with the resolved path, so scripts launched from autostart or
the root menu can rely on it being set.


Directional window focus
------------------------

The keyboard focus can now be moved in a specific direction (left, right,
up, down) relative to the currently focused window, using window centres
for comparison. The shortcuts can be configured in WPrefs under
"Keyboard Shortcuts", or set directly in ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker
via "FocusWindowLeftKey", "FocusWindowRightKey", "FocusWindowUpKey" and
"FocusWindowDownKey" (disabled by default).

The existing "CirculateRaise" option ("Raise window when switching focus
with keyboard" in WPrefs) also controls whether windows that are fully
obscured by others are raised when receiving focus this way.


Vim-like window marking
-----------------------

Windows can now be dynamically assigned a mark label (a key or
modifier+key combination) allowing them to be instantly recalled without
searching. This is inspired by vim's mark-and-jump navigation.

The mark label is displayed in the window list menu between the window
title and the workspace name.

Five actions can be configured in ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker
(or in WPrefs under "Keyboard Shortcuts")

   "MarkSetKey": assign a label to the focused window (e.g. with the
   action bound to "Super+M", pressing "Super+M x" marks the focused
   window with label 'x').

   "MarkUnsetKey": remove the mark from a labeled window.

   "MarkJumpKey": focus and raise the window with the given label.

   "MarkBringKey": move the marked window to the current workspace and
   focus it.

   "MarkSwapKey": swap the focused window with the marked window.

Those are disabled by default.
Marks are persistent across sessions and saved in the WMState file.


Multi-key and sticky-chain keybindings
--------------------------------------

Keyboard shortcuts now support multi-key sequences, similar to
Emacs-style prefix bindings (e.g. pressing a leader key followed by
one or more continuation keys).

An optional sticky-chain mode allows a prefix key to remain active
until a cancel key is pressed or a timeout expires, making it possible
to trigger multiple actions in sequence without re-entering the prefix.
New options in ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker:

   "KeychainTimeoutDelay" is the timeout in milliseconds after which an
   active chain expires. Default is 500. Set to 0 to disable the
   timeout.

   "KeychainCancelKey" sets a key to explicitly cancel an active chain.
   (disabled by default).

   "KeychainDisplayPosition" places a small popup on screen while a
   chain is active showing the keys typed so far, e.g. "Mod4+W" after
   the leader and "Mod4+W M" after a further prefix step. Accepts the
   same values as WorkspaceNameDisplayPosition (none, center, top,
   bottom, topleft, topright, bottomleft, bottomright). Default is
   "none" (disabled). The popup uses the same blue box style as the
   move/resize geometry display, which is now backed by a shared OSD
   widget so future overlays can reuse it.


Configurable modifier key labels
---------------------------------

A new "ModifierKeyShortLabels" option in ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker
allows customizing the labels shown for modifier keys in menus and
keyboard shortcut displays. Unicode symbols can be used in place of the
default text labels. For example to use the same symbol as defined on
MacOS:

ModifierKeyShortLabels = (
  "\342\207\247",
  "\342\214\203",
  "\342\214\245",
  "\342\207\255",
  "\342\207\263",
  "\342\214\230",
  "\342\207\252",
  "\342\227\206",
  "\342\214\245"
);

Configurable mouse wheel focus behavior
----------------------------------------

Scrolling the mouse wheel over a window can now optionally move the
keyboard focus to that window. The option is available in WPrefs under
"Expert User Preferences", or by setting "MouseWheelFocusEnable" in
~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker (NO by default).


Application icons in the window list
-------------------------------------

Application icons can now be shown in the window list menu. This behavior
can be configured by setting the "WindowListAppIcons" option in
~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker or "Show app icons in window list." in
WPrefs under "Expert User Preferences" (NO by default).


Configurable screenshot filename template
-----------------------------------------

The filename (strftime compatible) format used when saving screenshots
can now be customized by setting the "ScreenshotFileNameTemplate"
option in ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker. The default remains the
format introduced in 0.96.0.


JPEG XL image support
---------------------

WRaster can now optionally load JPEG XL images if the libjxl
library is present at build time. The feature can be toggled at
configure time with --enable-jxl/--disable-jxl.


wmiv: archive files and unknown format handling
-----------------------------------------------

wmiv can now open images stored inside archive files (with libarchive).
A new option to silently skip unknown image formats is also available.
It supports ctrl+c shortcut to copy the current image to the clipboard.


Support for _NET_WM_MOVERESIZE
------------------------------

Window Maker now handles the _NET_WM_MOVERESIZE EWMH message, which
allows applications to initiate interactive window moves and resizes
through a standard protocol request (for those applications without
decorations: VS Code, Google Chrome, Steam and Discord, ...).


Resize cursors on the resizebar
-------------------------------

The bottom resizebar now shows the bottom-left, vertical and
bottom-right resize cursors when the pointer hovers over its three
drag regions, so the resize direction is visible before the drag
starts instead of only once it has begun.


Cycle windows across all workspaces
-----------------------------------

A new 'Cycle all windows from all workspaces' expert option allows
the Alt-Tab window switcher to include windows from all workspaces,
not only the current one. Which is the "CycleAllWorkspaces" option
in ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker (NO by default).


RandR and multi-monitor improvements
------------------------------------

RandR version >= 1.3 is automatically enabled when detected. By default,
it operates in static mode, requiring the screen layout to be defined
manually. A new "HotplugMonitor" option in ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker
(NO by default) enables automatic monitor detection on hotplug events,
adding newly connected monitors to the right of the existing layout.
This option is also available in WPrefs expert panel under
"Automatically (de)activate monitors on hotplug events".


Titlebar language button revamp
-------------------------------

When the modelock feature is enabled, the legacy hardcoded language
dropdown icons in the titlebar are replaced by a compact button
displaying the short name of the current XKB locale (those are
basically the layouts displayed via setxkbmap -query).
Clicking the button cycles through up to four keyboard layouts
(matching the four XKB groups). The xkbfile library is required for
this feature.


-- 0.96.0

Hot Corners feature
-------------------

Screen corners can be assigned an external command to be
executed when the mouse pointer is entering those areas.
In WPrefs, "Hot Corner Shortcut Preferences" can be used
for configuration or by manually adding a "HotCorners" key
and value to "YES" in the ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker file.
Hot Corners feature is disabled by default.
Actions are specified by the "HotCornerActions" and are defined
as a four entries list ("top left action", "top right action",
"bottom left action", "bottom right action").
A screen corner area is a cube shape defined by the "HotCornerEdge"
which is a number of pixels from 2 (by default) to 10.
To lower the risk of triggering that feature accidentally a
"HotCornerDelay" key can be used which is the time before the action
is triggered while the pointer is in one of the screen corner.
Default value is 250 ms.


Screenshot capture feature
--------------------------

In WPrefs "Keyboard Shortcut Preferences" tab, three new actions can
be configured: "Capture a portion of the screen", "Capture a window",
"Capture the entire screen". The file is saved in
~/GNUstep/Library/WindowMaker/Screenshots directory under a filename
format "screenshot_%Y-%m-%d_at_%H:%M:%S" followed by the extension.
Which can be png or jpg based on WRaster dependencies.


libXRes is now an optional dependency
-------------------------------------

XRes the resource extension for the X protocol is used to find the
underlying processes (and PIDs) responsible for displaying the windows.


Support for _NET_WM_FULLSCREEN_MONITORS hint
--------------------------------------------

That hint allows applications that support it to be set as fullscreen on multiple heads.
It depends on Xinerama extension support.


Keep dock on primary head
-------------------------

To keep the dock on the primary head in a multi-head setup, set the option
"KeepDockOnPrimaryHead" in ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker to "YES" or click
"Keep dock on primary head" under the WPrefs "Expert User Preferences" tab.


-- 0.95.9

Configurable SwitchPanel
------------------------

SwitchPanel is now more configurable: you can configure the switch panel icon
size by setting the "SwitchPanelIconSize" option to your preferred value in
~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker. The font size used in this panel now is also
sensible to changes in the system font.


New user configuration directory environment variable
-----------------------------------------------------

In previous versions, the GNUstep directory used to store a user's Window Maker
configuration files was specified by the GNUSTEP_USER_ROOT environment variable,
which defaulted to ~/GNUstep.  However, this environment variable was deprecated
in gnustep-make v2.  Therefore, it has been replaced by the WMAKER_USER_ROOT
environment variable.


libXmu is now an optional dependency
------------------------------------

If the library is not found, compilation work, the only limitation will arise
when trying to install the standard colormap on displays which are not TrueColor.
Please note that if you have the library but not the headers, configure will
still stop; there is no user option to explicitly disable the library use.


-- 0.95.8

Move pointer with maximized windows
-----------------------------------

Implementation for moving mouse pointer within the maximized window.

Mouse pointer can be now moved together with window if keyboard was used for
arrange maximized windows on screen. This feature can be turned on in WPrefs.app
in Expert tab by selecting "Move mouse pointer with half maximized windows.", or
setting "PointerWithHalfMaxWindows" to "Yes" on ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker
file.


Alternative way for traverse half-maximized windows
---------------------------------------------------

For now, there could be three possible states of the window while using
the half-maximized feature. Unmaximized windows have their state saved during
that process, which is used to unmaximize them. For example, if there is a
window that is maximized on the left half of the screen, and while requesting
left-half-maximize it becomes unmaximized with its size and dimensions
restored to the original state.

By setting "AlternativeHalfMaximized" option to "Yes"
~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker config file (or by using WPrefs.app and option
"Alternative transitions between states for half maximized windows."), there
would be slightly different change in window "traverse". Given layout depicted
below:

 ┌┬────────────────────┬┐
 ├┘ ┌───────┐          ├┤
 │  ├───────┤          ├┤
 │  │       │          ├┤
 │  │       │          ├┤
 │  └───────┘          └┤
 ├┬┐                    │
 └┴┴────────────────────┘

Window can be moved using keyboard shortcut right-half-maximize:

 ┌┬─────────┬──────────┬┐
 ├┘         ├──────────┼┤
 │          │          ├┤
 │          │          ├┤
 │          │          ├┤
 │          │          ├┤
 ├┬┐        └──────────┘│
 └┴┴────────────────────┘

Further invoking right-half-maximize will do nothing. Note, that the window always
can be unmaximized using an appropriate keyboard shortcut or by selecting
"Unmaximize" from the window menu.

Going to opposite direction by invoking left-half-maximize, will make the window
maximized in both, vertical and horizontal directions:

 ┌─────────────────────┬┐
 ├─────────────────────┼┤
 │                     ├┤
 │                     ├┤
 │                     ├┤
 │                     ├┤
 ├┬┬───────────────────┘│
 └┴┴────────────────────┘

Further invoking left-half-maximize, will make the window maximized in half left
side od the screen:

 ┌──────────┬──────────┬┐
 ├──────────┤          ├┤
 │          │          ├┤
 │          │          ├┤
 │          │          ├┤
 │          │          └┤
 ├┬┬────────┘           │
 └┴┴────────────────────┘

And again, further invoking left-half-maximize, will do nothing in this mode.

This change affects all possible half-maximized window state also quarters.
Issuing bottom-left-corner will take lower left quarter of the screen (nothing
is new so far):

 ┌┬────────────────────┬┐
 ├┘                    ├┤
 │                     ├┤
 ├──────────┐          ├┤
 ├──────────┤          ├┤
 │          │          └┤
 ├┬┬────────┘           │
 └┴┴────────────────────┘

Issuing bottom-right-corner again will change window state to bottom half
maximized:

 ┌┬────────────────────┬┐
 ├┘                    ├┤
 │                     ├┤
 ├─────────────────────┼┤
 ├─────────────────────┼┤
 │                     ├┤
 ├┬┬───────────────────┘│
 └┴┴────────────────────┘

Invoking bottom-right-corner again:

 ┌┬────────────────────┬┐
 ├┘                    ├┤
 │                     ├┤
 │          ┌──────────┼┤
 │          ├──────────┼┤
 │          │          ├┤
 ├┬┐        └──────────┘│
 └┴┴────────────────────┘

Issuing bottom-right-corner again will have no effect.


Move half-maximized windows between the screens
-----------------------------------------------

New option was introduced to allow change of behaviour of half-maximize windows
in multiple displays environment. In such environment it is more natural that
half maximized windows would travel from one screen to another. Now it is
possible to make that happen by setting an option
"MoveHalfMaximizedWindowsBetweenScreens" to "Yes" in
~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker config file, or by checking "Allow move
half-maximized windows between multiple screens." in 'Expert User Preferences"
tab in WPrefs.app.

For example, given there are two screens in layout where one display is on the
left and second display is on the right with window on first screen:

 ┌┬────────────────┬─────────────────┬┐
 ├┘ ┌───────┐      │                 ├┤
 │  ├───────┤      │                 ├┤
 │  │       │      │                 ├┤
 │  │       │      │                 ├┤
 │  └───────┘      │                 └┤
 ├┬┐               ├┬┬┐               │
 └┴┴───────────────┴┴┴┴───────────────┘

It can be right-half-maximized (using the previously defined key combination), so it
becomes:

 ┌┬───────┬────────┬─────────────────┬┐
 ├┘       ├────────┤                 ├┤
 │        │        │                 ├┤
 │        │        │                 ├┤
 │        │        │                 ├┤
 │        │        │                 └┤
 ├┬┐      └────────┼┬┬┐               │
 └┴┴───────────────┴┴┴┴───────────────┘

In this example there is an assumption that WindowMaker is configured, that
maximized windows won't cover mini icons nor the dock.

Without setting new option to true, issuing another right-half-maximize will
restore window dimensions and position to the original state (like on the first
figure). With new option set to true it will move window to second screen like:

 ┌┬────────────────┬────────┬────────┬┐
 ├┘                ├────────┤        ├┤
 │                 │        │        ├┤
 │                 │        │        ├┤
 │                 │        │        ├┤
 │                 │        │        └┤
 ├┬┐               ├┬┬┬─────┘         │
 └┴┴───────────────┴┴┴┴───────────────┘

Another activation of right-half-maximize:

 ┌┬────────────────┬────────┬────────┬┐
 ├┘                │        ├────────┼┤
 │                 │        │        ├┤
 │                 │        │        ├┤
 │                 │        │        ├┤
 │                 │        │        ├┤
 ├┬┐               ├┬┬┐     └────────┘│
 └┴┴───────────────┴┴┴┴───────────────┘

And final activation of right-half-maximize:

 ┌┬────────────────┬───────┬─────────┬┐
 ├┘                ├───────┤         ├┤
 │                 │       │         ├┤
 │                 │       │         ├┤
 │                 ├───────┘         ├┤
 │                 │                 └┤
 ├┬┐               ├┬┬┐               │
 └┴┴───────────────┴┴┴┴───────────────┘

Where the window is restored to its size (but not position, since it is on a different
head now). Moving a window in directions like left-half-maximize,
top-half-maximize and bottom-half-maximize will behave in similar way depending
on the layout of displays.

Note, that only windows that are half-maximized vertically or horizontally can
be moved to another screen due to the fact, that direction of movement of
quarter-maximized windows is ambiguous. As for vertical/horizontal-maximize,
since doesn't move the window but only stretch it vertically/horizontally this
feature also doesn't apply.


Snapping a window to the top
----------------------------

You can now choose whether snapping a window to the top edge of the screen
maximizes it to the top half (the previous and default behavior) or to the
full screen by setting "SnapToTopMaximizesFullscreen" to "NO" or "YES",
respectively.  This setting can also be changed by unchecking or checking
"Snapping a window to the top maximizes it to the full screen" in the
"Expert User Preferences" tab in WPrefs.app.

Global defaults directory configuration
---------------------------------------

You can now configure the global defaults directory, where the system
WindowMaker, WMRootMenu, etc. files are stored, by running
    ./configure --with-defsdatadir=/path/to/defaults
at build time.  The previous method, which only partially worked, involved
defining the GLOBAL_DEFAULTS_SUBDIR macro.  This is no longer available.
Note that the default location is ${sysconfdir}/WindowMaker.

-- 0.95.7

Window snapping
---------------

You can now "snap" a window, i.e., maximize it to a side or corner of the
screen, by dragging it to that side or corner.  It is enabled by setting
"WindowSnapping = YES" in ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker or selecting "Maximize
(snap) a window to edge or corner by dragging." under "Expert User Preferences"
in WPrefs.app.

Note that if "Switch workspaces while dragging windows" is selected under
"Workspace Preferences" in WPrefs.app, or if "DontLinkWorkspaces = NO" in
~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker, then you may only snap a window to the top or
bottom of the screen.

You may set the distance (in pixels) from the edge or corner of the screen at
which a window will begin snapping using "SnapEdgeDetect" and "SnapCornerDetect"
in ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker or setting "Distance from edge/corner to begin
window snap." under "Expert User Preferences" in WPrefs.app.  (The defaults are
1 pixel and 10 pixels, respectively).


Dragging maximized windows
--------------------------
You can now control the behavior when a maximized window is dragged by setting
the "DragMaximizedWindow" option in ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker or by
selecting an option from the "When dragging a maximized window..." pop-up button
under "Window Handling Preferences" in WPrefs.app.

There are four choices:
* "Move" ("...change position (normal behavior)" in WPrefs.app) is the default
  and traditional behavior.  A maximized window is moved when dragged and
  remains maximized, i.e., it keeps its maximized geometry and can later be
  unmaximized.
* "RestoreGeometry" ("...restore unmaximized geometry") is the behavior standard
  in desktop environments like GNOME, Cinnamon, and Unity.  A maximized window
  is moved when dragged and is completely unmaximized, i.e., its unmaximized
  geometry is restored.
* "Unmaximize" ("...consider the window unmaximized") causes a maximized window
  to be moved when dragged and remains partially maximized, i.e., it keeps its
  maximized geometry, but is considered to be unmaximized.  In particular, it can
  be immediately re-maximized.
* "NoMove" ("...do not move the window") prevents a maximized window from being
  moved when dragged.

Note that, to accommodate this option in the "Window Handling Preferences" tab in
WPrefs.app, the option to "Open dialogs in the same workspace as their owners"
(which sets the "OpenTransientOnOwnerWorkspace" option from
~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker) has been moved to "Expert User Preferences".


Mini-Previews instead of Apercus
--------------------------------

Since the original name was not really clear because it is a French word that
is rarely used by British people, it was decided to change it to the more usual
Mini-Preview name. The setting is configurable with WPrefs in the Icon
Preferences tab, the size is now expressed in pixels directly.


Ignore Decoration Hints from GNOME applications
-----------------------------------------------

The GNOME applications ask Window Maker to get no title bar and no resize bar to
their windows by using "Hints". You can re-add them using the Attribute dialog
in the Window menu, but if you are using many GNOME applications you may want to
tell Window Maker to just ignore them. This is done with the new setting called
"IgnoreGtkHints", which is available in the "Expert" panel in WPrefs.


Cooperative Window Manager Replacement
--------------------------------------

The ICCCM defines a protocol for window managers to ask to replace the currently
running one; Window Maker now supports it. You can ask Window Maker to take the
place of current one by running "wmaker --replace", or any other window manager
can ask Window Maker to leave the place for them when started the same way.
Please note that this feature must be explicitely enabled at compile time because
by default it is not compiled in ("configure --enable-wmreplace").


--- 0.95.6

More image format supported
---------------------------

In addition to a more complete Netpbm image formats support, WindowMaker
can now load WebP images. It can also make use of the ImageMagick library
to support a lot more formats, like SVG, BMP, TGA...


Mini-window apercu
------------------

A small preview of window contents can be enabled from WPrefs,
in Miscellaneous Ergonomic Preferences, check miniwindow apercus.
Apercu size can be configured using the ApercuSize variable with

$ wdwrite WindowMaker ApercuSize 4

in multiples of the icon size (in this case the apercu size will be four
times the icon size).

The default size is 2 (twice the icon size).


Support for up to 9-buttons mouse
---------------------------------

The action for the newly supported buttons can be configured from WPrefs.


wmiv, an image viewer application
---------------------------------

wmiv is a quick image viewer using wrlib to be run from the command line.


--- 0.95.5

Support for generated menus in proplist format
----------------------------------------------

The root menu now supports a OPEN_PLMENU option to construct a submenu from
the output of a command which is proplist format. This can be used e.g. in
conjunction with wmmenugen like:

(
    "Generated PL Submenu", OPEN_PLMENU,
    "|| find /usr/share/applications -type f -name '*desktop' | xargs wmmenugen -parser:xdg"
)


New window placements
---------------------

Now it is possible to maximize windows to the top/bottom halves
of the screen and also to the corners (top/bottom + left/right).

The keyboard shortcuts can be configured with WPrefs.


Options to configure window/menu borders
----------------------------------------

You can now configure the width and color of window and menu borders.
For example, the default settings could be configured as follows:

$ wdwrite WindowMaker FrameBorderWidth 1
$ wdwrite WindowMaker FrameBorderColor black
$ wdwrite WindowMaker FrameSelectedBorderColor white


Keyboard shortcuts to move windows between workspaces
-----------------------------------------------------

You can now bind keyboard shortcuts - or use the window dropdown menus -
to move windows to other workspaces.  You can either move a window directly to
a particular workspace or to the "next" or "previous" workspace.

The new shortcuts can be configured in WPrefs.


Native support for Drawers
--------------------------

WindowMaker now supports drawers in the dock natively. You can add a dock by
right-clicking on a docked appicon and select "Add a drawer". You can now drag
appicons to this drawer.
You can customize how you want your drawers to auto-expand/collapse and
auto-raise/lower, or you can also completely disable them.


Improved switch panel functionality
-----------------------------------

The switch panel can be used to switch between windows of the same WM_CLASS,
for example between all open xterms.  If the switch panel is opened as normal
with either the "FocusNextKey" or "FocusPrevKey" shortcut, you can switch to
the next (or previous) window of the same type as the focused window with the
"GroupNextKey" or "GroupPrevKey" shortcut.  If the switch panel is opened with
the "GroupNextKey" or "GroupPrevKey" shortcut, it will show only windows of
the same type as the window which was focused at the time the panel was opened,
and no difference will be seen between the two types of window selection
shortcut.

The new shortcuts can be configured in WPrefs, where they are described as
allowing switching between windows of the same group.

To maintain consistency with other popular operating systems, the switch panel
is now configured so that it no longer automatically closes when the shift key
is pressed and released.

To configure the switch panel so that it does close on release of the shift
key, which was the traditional Window Maker behavior, run the following
command:

$ wdwrite WindowMaker StrictWindozeCycling NO

If you find yourself regularly opening the switch panel just to visualize open
windows, you can run the following command to force the first "FocusNextKey"
or similar shortcut to open the panel without switching to a new window.

$ wdwrite WindowMaker SwitchPanelOnlyOpen YES


--- 0.95.4

New window placement strategy
-----------------------------

Among the window placement algorithms has arrived the "center" placement to
get new windows appear at the center of the screen (unless application
explicitly specify a position, of course).


Removed dependency to CPP
-------------------------

The menu files used to be pre-processed with CPP, which can be a problem because
modern system do not install dev tools by default, and some compilers do not
provide a pre-processor anyway. WindowMaker is now autonomous.


--- 0.95.3

The references to the legacy path /usr/X11R6 have been removed because xorg
does not use it in favor of a standard /usr layout. Similarly, the directory
/etc/X11/WindowMaker is now /usr/share/WindowMaker.


New application Relaunching functionality
-----------------------------------------

There are now several ways to launch a new instance of an application with the
same command line that was originally used to start it.

1. By selecting Launch from the application's window menu.
2. By using the "Launch new instance of application" keyboard shortcut.
3. By clicking the application's appicon with the middle button.
4. By double-clicking the application's appicon while holding Control.

For example, if you have two xterms open, one started with "xterm" and one
started with "xterm -rv", using the Relaunch functionality on the first xterm
would run "xterm" and using it on the second would run "xterm -rv".  Thus
Relaunching can also be thought of as "cloning" an application.

Application Relaunching works by examining the window's WM_COMMAND property and
so will not work if it is not set.


Options to limit the window/menu title height
---------------------------------------------

You can now set the minimum and maximum titlebar heights.
For example, to force all titlebars to 24 pixels execute
the following commands:

$ wdwrite WindowMaker WindowTitleMinHeight 24
$ wdwrite WindowMaker WindowTitleMaxHeight 24
$ wdwrite WindowMaker MenuTitleMinHeight 24
$ wdwrite WindowMaker MenuTitleMaxHeight 24


--- 0.95.2

New Resizing functionality
--------------------------

You can now use the mouse wheel and modifier keys to resize windows.
MOD+Wheel will resize windows vertically and CTRL+Wheel will resize
windows horizontally. MOD+CTRL+Wheel will resize both at the same time.
The resize step, or increment can be set in WPrefs in the "Window Handling"
page, or in the config file with "ResizeIncrement". If this intrudes into
a specific application's functionality, you can disable it on an application-
by-application basis by setting the "Do not bind mouse clicks" attribute in
"Advanced Options".

New Maximize functionality
--------------------------

Maximus/Maximumize. A new tiled maximization, configured with a keyboard
shortcut. Using it will maximize the window to the greatest area such that it
will not overlap any other window of the same workspace. This can be configured
in WPrefs in the "Keyboard Shortcuts" page, or in the config file with
"MaximusKey".

Left/right maximization. Now wmaker supports maximizing a window to the
left or right half of the screen, making it occupy 50% of the area. This is
useful on wide screen displays where you want to bring up two windows side-by-
side. The shortcut keys for this can be configured in WPrefs in the "Keyboard
Shortcuts" page, or in the config file with "LHMaximizeKey" and "RHMaximizeKey"

History and AutoComplete in the run dialog
------------------------------------------

The "Run..." dialog now auto-completes executables in your path (triggered by
the TAB key) and records the history of past commands (last 500 by default).
It can also complete folder names if you start the complete with a "/"
To use it replace %a with %A in the "Run..." entry in your WMRootMenu file.
See commit 05720d97076ffc1569e5 for more details.

Automatic menu generator (wmgenmenu)
-----------------------------------

There is now a new utility to generate the Window Maker menu automatically,
called wmgenmenu. It searches from a list of pre-defined applications the
ones which exist in your $PATH and adds them to the corresponding submenus
("Internet", "Terminals", "Editors" etc) of your WM menu. It also supports
localization (currently English and German). You can use it like:

$ wmgenmenu > WMRootMenu-new
$ cp WMRootMenu-new $HOME/GNUstep/Defaults/WMRootMenu

and WM will automatically detect the new menu file and use it without
restarting wmaker (made possible by the 'inotify' mechanism)

Automatic detection of menu changes (via inotify)
-------------------------------------------------

Window Maker now uses the 'inotify' mechanism from the Linux kernel
to automatically detect changes in the WMRootMenu file. So the
--no-polling option is now gone and Window Maker does not wake up
your CPU unnecessarily (0 wakeups when idle, instead of 4).
So if you edit the WMRootMenu file by hand (or by using 'wmgenmenu'),
there is no need to restart wmaker for the changes to take effect.

DockApp recognition
-------------------

In addition to applications with only Withdrawn windows, Window Maker
will now treat any application with its WM_CLASS res_class set as
"DockApp". This provides an easy workaround for toolkits like gtk+ that
do not allow creation of windows with the initial_state member of
XWMHints set to WithdrawnState.


--- 0.92.0

GNUstep Installation Directory
------------------------------

WPrefs is now installed in /usr/local/bin and /usr/local/share by default.
If you use GNUstep and want it to install in /usr/GNUstep/Applications,
you may specify --with-gnustepdir=/usr/GNUstep
If the GNUSTEP_LOCAL_ROOT environment variable is defined when configure is
executed, it will be used (and you don't need to use --with-gnustepdir)

Cached Pixmaps Directory
------------------------

The directory where Window Maker stores the cached application pixmaps for
its later use has changed from ~/GNUstep/.AppInfo/WindowMaker to
~/GNUstep/Library/WindowMaker/CachedPixmaps for better compatibility with
the GNUstep path structure.
Also WPrefs now stores internal data in ~/GNUstep/Library/WindowMaker/WPrefs
(it was ~/GNUstep/.AppInfo/WPrefs before)

X Input Methods support in WINGs
--------------------------------

Preliminary support for X Input Methods was added to textfield and text
widgets in WINGs. Input for text in other languages than English should
work now (except for kanji which will most likely not work, even though
it wasn't tested).


Disabling the switch panel
--------------------------

To disable the panel shown during Alt-tabbing, you may put the following in
~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker

SwitchPanelImages= None;



--- 0.91.0

Alt-Tab Window Switching
------------------------

You can change the appearance of the panel shown during Alt-Tab window switching
with the SwitchPanelImages option:

 (selected_icon_tile_image, background_image, width, height)

selected_icon_tile_image is the image used to highlight the currently selected
window icon. It must be 64x64 pixels.

background_image is the image used in the background of the panel. It must
be at least 64x80.

width and height are the width and size of the central part of the image.
When drawing the panel, the image will be split as:
                           |W |
			+--+--+--+
			|  |  |  |
			+--+--+--+ -
			|  |  |  | H
			+--+--+--+ -
			|  |  |  |
			+--+--+--+

The 4 corner images will be copied in their original sizes and the rest will
be scaled to the final panel size.

background_image, width and height are optional. If you leave them out,
a gray panel will be used. If your machine is not very fast, you may want
to use it.


--- 0.90.0


NetWM / EWMH Support
--------------------

Support for the EWMH standard has been added. Applications from GNOME 2.x and
KDE 3.x should now inter-operate better with Window Maker.

Support for the obsolete/legacy GNOME 1.x, KDE 1.x and OpenLook(!) hints was
wiped out for the sake of sanity.


Antialiased font support
------------------------

With the addition of Xft2 support in the WINGs library, now
Window Maker can display antialiased text with TrueType or any scalable fonts.

You can pick fonts for Window Maker in the Font configuration section of
WPrefs.

Antialiased text is enabled by default, but can be disabled by adding

AntialiasedText = NO; in ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WMGLOBAL

This will disable antialiased text for any WINGs based application. If you
only want to disable them for a specific application only, like WindowMaker
for example, then add the same option in the applications configuration file,
in this case ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WindowMaker
For WindowMaker, this can also be achieved from the Expert panel in WPrefs.

Note that bitmapped fonts look much better than TrueType when antialiasing is
disabled.

Global Submenus
---------------

Global menus allow for system wide menus that are added to every users
application menus. They are located in /usr/etc/WindowMaker/,
/usr/local/etc/WindowMaker or whatever is your sysconf directory
for WindowMaker. There are 2 files:

GlobalMenu.pre, which is added to the beginning of the menu and
GlobalMenu.post, which is added to the end of the menu.

These are to be proplist format menus, for example:

(("Foobar", EXEC, foobar),
 ("Blabla", EXEC, blabla))

or, in case you want a submenu:

(("Submenu",
	("Foobar", EXEC, foobar),
	("Blabla", EXEC, blabla)))


UTF-8 Support
-------------

Window Maker now uses UTF-8 internally (and thus can display UTF-8 text
in window titles and other places). Menus and po files must now be
encoded in UTF-8. If your menus contain non ASCII characters, you can convert
them to UTF-8 with the following command:

iconv -f <current-file-encoding> -t utf-8 <filename> > <filename>.utf8

For example:

iconv -f iso-8859-1 -t utf-8 menu > menu.utf
mv menu.utf menu


Icon Panel for Alt-Tabbing
--------------------------

A panel with icons for the windows that you can switch to will appear
when you press Alt-Tab. You can navigate through the windows with Alt-Tab
(Alt-Shift-Tab) or with the left/right keys once the panel is shown.



--- 0.80.0

Shading/Unshading windows using mouse wheel on their titlebar
-------------------------------------------------------------

In addition to the known methods of shading/unshading a window,  one can now
do this by using the mouse wheel on the window's titlebar. The mouse events
are interpreted via a mapping in the global WINGs configuration file,
WMGLOBAL, by the MouseWheelUp and MouseWheelDown directives which will do
shading and unshading respectfully.

However, to avoid unwanted triggers of shading/unshading the window, two
consecutive mouse wheel events in the same direction are required. The
trigger won't occur if the events are separated by more than a double-click's
worth of time, which is technically speaking like making a double-click with
the button that corresponds to the mouse wheel direction.

Practically speaking, this means that you have to move the mouse wheel up
or down quickly, like when you want to quickly scroll over something big.


Shared application icons
------------------------

Real application icon sharing was implemented in place of the collapse
appicon thing. With this applications of the same instance.class will
have a single shared application icon and hiding will hide all windows
of the application attached to that appicon as if there is a single
application. This feature is enabled by default for all applications in
the global WMWindowAttributes defaults domain using:

"*" = {SharedAppIcon = Yes;};

If you're not satisfied with this or want the old behavior back you
can revert this (either in the global domain for all users or in your
personal WMWindowAttibutes domain) using SharedAppIcon = No; for "*"
It can also be enabled/disabled for individual applications as needed.

Setting this option can be done using the window's inspector panel in the
"Application Specific" section. You can set/unset it for all applications by
using the "Defaults for all windows" in the "Window Specification" section

Basically using this can have 2 major scenarios:
1. Leave it on by default, but disable it for the few specific
   applications that do not behave well with it. (default)
2. Leave it off by default, and enable it for all applications for
   which you want it enabled.

At this point all applications we tested work ok, some of them even
work better with this feature turned on: for example xmms and Corel's
WorkPerfect8 now only have 1 appicon (they used to have 2 without this
feature).

This feature is turned off by default for the following applications
because it's incompatible with them:
1. all GNUstep applications
2. applications with an application menu (wterm, Aileron, etc)
3. all applications with withdrawn windows only (this means all dockapps)

As a side note: wterm can use the shared appicon feature as long as it
doesn't use the appmenu (will do this by default). If you start it
using the appmenu (wterm -wm) it will disable the shared appicon
feature because apps with appmenus are incompatible with this feature.

If an application is a GNUstep application or if it has an appmenu, it's
detected automatically and the shared appicon is disabled automatically
without any user intervention or need to configure anything.


Dock/Clip stealing appicons
---------------------------

This feature is different form the Clip's "Autoattract Icons" feature
as it won't attach any new icon to the dock/clip.
What it does, is when you start an application by other means than
dock/clip, like for example using the main menu or a terminal, it will
search the dock/clips for the presence of an appicon for that
application, that is not already running at that moment and will attach
the started application to that appicon in the dock/clip if available,
making it look like the dock/clip just stole the appicon for the started
application. There is an animation for this to offer visual feedback
that this happened.



